const TREKS: Record<string, string> = {"agasthyarkoodam": "## Agasthyarkoodam\n- **ID**: `agasthyarkoodam`\n- **Location**: Western Ghats, Kerala, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 3 days | **Max Altitude**: 1868m\n- **Best Months**: 1, 2, 3\n\n**Description**: Embark on a highly restricted, deeply spiritual ascent into the mist-shrouded Western Ghats, trailing through dense canopies bursting with rare medicinal herbs to the sacred peak of Agasthya Muni.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Highly restricted, untouched biosphere reserve access\n- Trails lined with rare Ayurvedic medicinal plants\n- Summiting the sacred peak revered by multiple faiths\n- Passing through the vibrant, seasonal Neelakurinji habitat\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive to Bonacaud and Trek to Athirumala (1000m, 14 km / Trek)\n  Your journey begins at the Bonacaud forest picket station, plunging almost immediately into a thick, emerald canopy. The trail winds through dense elephant grass and towering tropical trees, accompanied by the symphony of endemic birdlife, until you reach the isolated forest camp at Athirumala.\n  *Bonacaud*: An old, misty tea-estate village serving as the last motorable point and gateway to the Agasthyamala Biosphere Reserve.\n- Day 2 — Summit Agasthyarkoodam and Return to Athirumala (1868m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Wake before dawn for the arduous but spiritually rewarding summit push. The trail transforms from dense forest into exposed, rocky rock faces requiring careful scrambling. Reaching the peak, you are greeted by the serene idol of Sage Agasthya and sweeping, cloud-piercing views of the Western Ghats.\n  *Athirumala Camp*: The sole, rudimentary forest department basecamp offering safe shelter in this wild, animal-rich territory.\n- Day 3 — Descend to Bonacaud (600m, 14 km / Trek + Drive)\n  Retrace your steps back through the vibrant green undergrowth. The descent feels faster, allowing you to fully absorb the medicinal fragrance of the air and the filtered sunlight piercing the canopy before emerging back at civilization in Bonacaud.\n  *Trivandrum*: The nearby capital city where you'll return to rest your legs and celebrate the completion of the pilgrimage.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Agasthyarkoodam: The Sacred Peak of the Western Ghats", "ama-dablam-bc": "## Ama Dablam Base Camp\n- **ID**: `ama-dablam-bc`\n- **Location**: Khumbu, Solukhumbu, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 4600m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Stand at the foot of the Himalayas' most architecturally stunning mountain, tracing ancient Sherpa trails to an amphitheater of ice and granite.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Unobstructed, dramatic views of the iconic Ama Dablam peak\n- Quieter trails compared to the bustling Everest Base Camp route\n- Breathtaking base camp setting surrounded by glacial streams\n- Excellent acclimatization opportunities and authentic Sherpa village stays\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Ama Dablam BC base (2610m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Travel to the trailhead, feeling the crisp mountain air fill your lungs as the valley opens up. Briefing and gear check at the lodge.\n  *Ama Dablam BC Base*: The starting point and gateway village; bustling with trekkers and lined with local shops for last-minute supplies.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3406m, 10 km)\n  Wind your way through fragrant pine forests and across suspension bridges draped in colorful prayer flags. The trail climbs steadily, revealing your first glimpses of the high peaks.\n  *Camp 2*: A scenic campsite perched at 3406m, offering incredible sunset views over the lower Khumbu valley.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3804m, 10 km)\n  The treeline begins to thin, replaced by rugged shrubs and sweeping alpine tundra. You'll pass intricate mani stones carved with Buddhist mantras as you ascend.\n  *Camp 3*: Nestled at 3804m, this camp provides crisp, thin air and proximity to grazing yaks and remote herder huts.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (4202m, 5 km)\n  A shorter but steeper day as you transition into the true high-alpine environment. The imposing silhouette of Ama Dablam begins to dominate the skyline.\n  *Camp 4*: A rocky, high-altitude sanctuary at 4202m, serving as your final launchpad before the base camp push.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (4600m, 6 km)\n  The crescendo of the trek. You cross moraines and glacial streams to reach the sprawling, majestic meadow of Ama Dablam Base Camp. Stand in awe directly beneath the mountain's towering, fluted ice faces.\n  *Ama Dablam BC High Camp*: An exposed, spectacular amphitheater at 4600m where mountaineers prepare to conquer the \"Matterhorn of the Himalayas.\"\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 4 (4202m, 7 km)\n  Leave the icy shadows of the base camp and begin your descent. The increased oxygen is a welcome relief as you return to the familiar rocky terrain.\n  *Camp 4*: Enjoy a restful evening at 4202m, reflecting on the sheer scale of the peak you just touched.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 3 (3804m, 5 km)\n  A relatively easy downhill walk. The landscape softens as you descend back into the upper reaches of the shrub layer.\n  *Camp 3*: A comfortable night at 3804m, where the air feels remarkably thick compared to the previous days.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 2 (3406m, 5 km)\n  Plunge back into the treeline, where the smell of damp earth and pine needles rejuvenates the senses. \n  *Camp 2*: Relax at 3406m, enjoying the warmth of lower elevations and hearty local meals.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 1 (3008m, 10 km)\n  A long, scenic descent following the roaring glacial rivers. The trail is shared with trains of mules carrying supplies up the valley.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final wilderness camp at 3008m, surrounded by the lively sounds of the forest and rushing water.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (2610m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final stretch brings you back to the vibrant trailhead village. A celebratory meal and a hot shower await!\n  *Ama Dablam BC Base*: Return to the base village. Celebrate your incredible 10-day trek completion!\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Ama Dablam Base Camp: At the Foot of the Jewel", "annapurna-panorama": "## Annapurna Panorama (Royal Trek)\n- **ID**: `annapurna-panorama`\n- **Location**: Annapurna, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 4 days | **Max Altitude**: 2100m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Walk the exact trails trodden by Prince Charles in the 1980s, tracing a lush, terraced ridge line that serves up sweeping vistas of the Annapurna Massif.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Royal heritage: The trail famously walked by British royalty\n- Gentle, easy ridge walks ideal for beginners and families\n- Uninterrupted, sweeping panoramic views of the Annapurnas and Machapuchare\n- Immersive experiences in traditional Gurung farming villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Royal Trek base (820m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A short drive from Pokhara brings you to the trailhead. You'll stretch your legs with a brief 3km walk through terraced fields to your first stop.\n  *Royal Trek Base*: A charming gateway village in the lower foothills, offering glimpses of the rural Nepali lifestyle.\n- Day 2 — Summit / High Point (2100m, 6 km)\n  The trail climbs gently through rhododendron forests and vibrant agricultural terraces to the trek's highest point. At the summit, the magnificent Annapurna range erupts across the horizon.\n  *Royal Trek High Camp*: The scenic apex of the trek at 2100m. A breathtaking vantage point offering panoramic views of the Himalayas.\n- Day 3 — Descend to Camp 1 (1460m, 5 km)\n  Begin your descent along the ridge, passing through serene Gurung villages where locals dry corn and millet in the sun. The mountain views stay with you for most of the walk.\n  *Camp 1*: A tranquil campsite or teahouse stop at 1460m, surrounded by the warmth of lower-altitude flora.\n- Day 4 — Return to base (820m, 8 km + drive)\n  Wind down through the final forests and farmlands to the valley floor, where your transport waits to whisk you back to the lakeside comforts of Pokhara.\n  *Royal Trek Base*: Return to the starting point. Celebrate your easy, rewarding Himalayan foray!\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Annapurna Panorama (Royal Trek): A Walk Fit for a King", "buran-ghati": "## Buran Ghati\n- **ID**: `buran-ghati`\n- **Location**: Shimla-Kinnaur, Himachal Pradesh, India\n- **Difficulty**: Difficult | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 4572m\n- **Best Months**: 5, 6, 9, 10\n\n**Description**: Ascend through the sprawling meadows of Dayara to a sheer 4,575m snow wall, culminating in an adrenaline-pumping 45-meter fixed-rope rappel into the Kinnaur valley.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- The exhilarating 45-meter fixed-rope rappel descent from Buran Ghati Pass\n- Serene, turquoise waters of the sacred Chandranahan Lakes\n- Panoramic views of the Dhauladhar and Kinnaur Kailash ranges\n- Camping in the vast, blooming alpine meadows of Dayara Thatch and Litham\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive to Janglik, The Gateway to Buran Ghati (2800m, Drive 4-5 hrs)\n  Your adventure begins with a scenic drive from Shimla to Janglik, a picturesque village nestled in the Pabbar Valley. The drive itself is a journey through charming Himachali landscapes. Upon arrival, settle into your guesthouse and take a short acclimatization walk.\n  *Janglik*: A quaint, remote village known for its traditional wooden houses and apple orchards, offering the last road connectivity.\n- Day 2 — Janglik to Dayara Thatch: Into the Wilderness (3400m, 8 km)\n  The trail winds through dense pine and oak forests, hugging the Pabbar River. You'll emerge from the tree line into vast, open meadows. Dayara Thatch is a sprawling shepherd's paradise, offering spectacular views and a deep sense of wilderness.\n  *Dayara Thatch*: A beautiful, expansive meadow system providing stunning panoramic views of the surrounding ranges.\n- Day 3 — Dayara Thatch to Litham: Approaching Chandranahan (3600m, 6 km)\n  Traverse more expansive meadows and cross mountain streams. The views become more dramatic, with the mountains seeming to close in. Litham is a stunning campsite acting as a crucial acclimatization stop.\n  *Litham*: A high-altitude camping ground situated near the source of the Pabbar River.\n- Day 4 — Exploration Day: Chandranahan Lakes (4000m, 4 km round trip)\n  Trek to the stunning Chandranahan Lakes, a cluster of seven sacred glacial tarns. The main lake, partially frozen even in summer, is the source of the Pabbar River. Return to Litham for a 'climb high, sleep low' acclimatization strategy.\n  *Chandranahan Lakes*: A revered cluster of high-altitude glacial lakes nestled in a dramatic rocky basin.\n- Day 5 — Litham to Dhunda: The Pass Approach (4100m, 6 km)\n  Push towards the base of Buran Ghati. The terrain becomes rockier and more exposed, with frequent snow patches. Dhunda is strategically located to launch our summit attempt the next day; the air will feel noticeably thinner here.\n  *Dhunda*: A rugged, high-altitude campsite on a glacial moraine, surrounded by stark mountain scenery.\n- Day 6 — Buran Ghati Pass and Rappel to River Camp (4572m Pass / 3500m Camp, 12 km)\n  A pre-dawn start takes you up a steep snow climb to Buran Ghati Pass (4,572m). Feast on panoramic views of Kinnaur Kailash before facing the trek's highlight: a thrilling 45-meter fixed-rope rappel down an ice wall. Post-rappel, descend over loose scree to River Camp.\n  *River Camp*: A basic but incredibly welcome campsite nestled beside a glacial stream on the Kinnaur side.\n- Day 7 — River Camp to Sangla, Drive to Shimla (2600m, 8 km trek, Drive 8-9 hrs)\n  The final day offers a rewarding descent through the picturesque landscapes of Kinnaur. The trail mellows out, passing through apple orchards into Sangla. Board a vehicle for the long drive back to Shimla.\n  *Sangla*: A vibrant village in the Baspa Valley renowned for its stunning scenery and traditional Kinnauri architecture.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Buran Ghati: The Thrill of the Rappel", "chadar": "## Chadar Trek\n- **ID**: `chadar`\n- **Location**: Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 9 days | **Max Altitude**: 3850m\n- **Best Months**: 1, 2\n\n**Description**: Walk on the frozen, glass-like surface of the Zanskar River through deep, echoing canyons in -25°C temperatures, tracing the historic winter trade route of Zanskari villagers.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trekking on a frozen river through towering, dramatic rock gorges\n- Surviving and thriving in an extreme winter expedition at -25°C\n- Discovering frozen waterfalls suspended mid-cascade\n- Experiencing the ultimate test of mental and physical endurance in Ladakh\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive in Leh and Acclimatize (3500m, Drive)\n  Touch down in Leh, where the brutal winter cold hits you immediately. Spend the day indoors sipping butter tea, adjusting to the 3500m altitude. \n  *Leh*: The capital of Ladakh, transformed into a quiet, frozen outpost during the harsh winter months.\n- Day 2 — Medical Check-up and Acclimatization in Leh (3500m)\n  Mandatory medical clearance is required by the local administration before attempting the Chadar. Spend the rest of the day gathering last-minute extreme weather gear.\n  *Leh*: Ensure your body is adapting to the thin, freezing air before venturing into the wilderness.\n- Day 3 — Drive to Shingra Koma and Trek to Tsomo Paldar (3200m, Drive 3 hrs + Trek 3 km)\n  Drive past the confluence of the Indus and Zanskar rivers. You step onto the ice for the first time at Shingra Koma, learning the \"penguin shuffle\" to avoid slipping, walking a short distance to your first riverbank camp.\n  *Tsomo Paldar*: Your first campsite on a sandy bend of the frozen river, surrounded by towering canyon walls.\n- Day 4 — Trek from Tsomo Paldar to Tibb Cave (3300m, 14 km)\n  A long day walking on the \"Chadar\" (blanket of ice). The ice formations change constantly—sometimes thick and white, sometimes glass-clear revealing the dark river below. You'll pass frozen waterfalls glistening in the sun.\n  *Tibb Cave*: A traditional campsite where Zanskari traders have sheltered for centuries in the natural grottos along the river.\n- Day 5 — Trek from Tibb Cave to Nerak (3400m, 12 km)\n  The canyon narrows spectacularly today. The highlight is arriving at Nerak, where a mammoth 50-foot frozen waterfall plummets down the rock face—the iconic postcard image of the Chadar Trek.\n  *Nerak*: The coldest point on the trek. A small Zanskari village lies 2,000 feet above the campsite.\n- Day 6 — Return Trek from Nerak to Tibb Cave (3300m, 12 km)\n  Begin the return journey. The river dynamic changes daily; ice that was solid yesterday might be broken today, forcing you to scramble over rocks on the banks.\n  *Tibb Cave*: Return to the familiar shelter of the caves, feeling more confident on the ice.\n- Day 7 — Trek from Tibb Cave to Shingra Koma and Drive to Leh (3200m, 17 km + Drive)\n  The final long push on the ice. Take your last mental pictures of the majestic Zanskar gorge before stepping off the ice and boarding the vehicles back to the comforts of Leh.\n  *Leh*: A hot shower and a warm bed await you after days of surviving in sub-zero wilderness.\n- Day 8 — Buffer Day in Leh (3500m)\n  The Chadar is highly unpredictable. This buffer day is essential in case bad weather, broken ice, or road blockages delay the trek schedule.\n  *Leh*: Use this day to explore Leh Palace or the Shanti Stupa in its winter glory.\n- Day 9 — Departure from Leh (3500m)\n  Board your flight out of the Himalayas, carrying with you the bragging rights of having completed India's most extreme winter expedition.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Chadar Trek: The Frozen River Expedition", "cho-la-pass": "## Cho La Pass\n- **ID**: `cho-la-pass`\n- **Location**: Khumbu, Solukhumbu, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Difficult | **Duration**: 14 days | **Max Altitude**: 5420m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Ditch the crowded main trail and cross a formidable 5,420m glaciated pass, linking the emerald lakes of Gokyo with the ultimate prize of Everest Base Camp.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Crossing the technical, glaciated Cho La Pass at 5,420m\n- Linking two legendary regions: Gokyo Valley and Everest Base Camp\n- Incredible views of Ama Dablam, Cholatse, and Makalu\n- Walking across the sprawling Ngozumpa Glacier, the longest in Nepal\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Cho La Pass base (2610m, Drive + 3 km)\n  After a thrilling flight to Lukla, the trek begins with a gentle descent towards the Dudh Kosi river, crossing swaying suspension bridges.\n  *Cho La Pass Base*: Phakding (or similar starting gateway), a vibrant village serving as the first stop on the Everest trail.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3413m, 11 km)\n  A steep climb up the valley brings you into the Sagarmatha National Park. You cross the famous Hillary Suspension Bridge before the final, heart-pumping ascent into the Sherpa capital.\n  *Camp 2*: Namche Bazaar, the bustling, horseshoe-shaped hub of the Khumbu region.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3814m, 9 km)\n  Leaving the main EBC highway, the trail climbs toward the quieter Gokyo valley, passing through rhododendron forests with sweeping views of Thamserku.\n  *Camp 3*: Dole or Phortse Thanga, quiet settlements offering a taste of high-altitude tranquility.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (4216m, 9 km)\n  The trees vanish, replaced by barren alpine scrub. You climb steadily along the valley wall, gaining significant altitude.\n  *Camp 4*: Machhermo, a small herding settlement where you'll feel the dramatic drop in temperature.\n- Day 5 — Ascend to Camp 5 (4617m, 6 km)\n  A spectacular day as you hike past the first and second Gokyo lakes, their turquoise waters contrasting sharply with the white snow and grey rock.\n  *Camp 5*: Gokyo village, situated beautifully on the shores of the third lake, beneath the looming Ngozumpa Glacier.\n- Day 6 — Ascend to Camp 6 (5019m, 7 km)\n  Cross the massive, moraine-covered Ngozumpa Glacier. The path shifts constantly due to the moving ice. Once across, you position yourself for the pass.\n  *Camp 6*: Dragnag (Thangnak), a remote outpost serving purely as a staging ground for the Cho La crossing.\n- Day 7 — Summit Cho La Pass (5420m, 6 km)\n  An early, brutal start. You scramble up steep rock and scree before hitting the glaciated section of the pass. Microspikes are often needed here. The top (5420m) offers mind-bending views of Cholatse before a steep descent.\n  *Cho La Pass High Camp*: The icy, windswept saddle of the pass itself.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 6 (5019m, 7 km)\n  Wait, the data provided shows a symmetric descent down the *exact* same elevations (5019m, 4617m, etc.). On a standard circuit, you would descend to Dzongla (4830m) and then head to Lobuche. *Following the exact prompt data*: You descend to 5019m.\n  *Camp 6*: A high-altitude rest stop to recover from the grueling pass crossing.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 5 (4617m, 7 km)\n  Continue losing altitude as you merge back toward the main trekking thoroughfares of the Khumbu.\n  *Camp 5*: A welcoming lodge stop at 4617m, where oxygen begins to feel slightly more plentiful.\n- Day 10 — Descend to Camp 4 (4216m, 10 km)\n  A long day of downhill trekking, retracing paths through the alpine scrubland.\n  *Camp 4*: Enjoy the relative warmth of 4216m.\n- Day 11 — Descend to Camp 3 (3814m, 5 km)\n  Drop back into the treeline, where the smell of juniper and pine returns to the air.\n  *Camp 3*: A peaceful evening at 3814m.\n- Day 12 — Descend to Camp 2 (3413m, 6 km)\n  Return to the bustling atmosphere of Namche Bazaar, where bakeries and hot showers await.\n  *Camp 2*: Celebrate your success in the Khumbu's capital.\n- Day 13 — Descend to Camp 1 (3011m, 11 km)\n  The long descent down the Namche hill and out of the high national park.\n  *Camp 1*: A riverside camp at 3011m.\n- Day 14 — Return to base (2610m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final walk back to Lukla, concluding the epic circuit. \n  *Cho La Pass Base*: Return to the airstrip. Celebrate your trek completion!\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Cho La Pass: The High Alpine Bridge", "deo-tibba": "## Deo Tibba Base Camp\n- **ID**: `deo-tibba`\n- **Location**: Kullu, Himachal Pradesh, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 6 days | **Max Altitude**: 4460m\n- **Best Months**: 5, 6, 9, 10\n\n**Description**: Wander through the ancient, temple-studded village of Jagatsukh into a breathtaking amphitheater of alpine meadows and sweeping glacial moraines at the foot of Deo Tibba.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Deo Tibba Peak Expedition base access\n- Sprawling, star-lit green pastures of Seri\n- Traditional Himachali architecture in Jagatsukh\n- Immersive walks along pristine mountain streams\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive from Manali to Jagatsukh & Trek to Khanol (2500m, 6 km / Drive + Trek)\n  Your journey ignites with a picturesque drive from bustling Manali to the historic village of Jagatsukh. Leaving the road behind, the trail plunges into lush, forested landscapes, winding upward to your first serene camp.\n  *Khanol*: A tranquil, forested campsite nestled amidst towering pines, offering a peaceful first night under the Himalayan sky.\n- Day 2 — Khanol to Chikka (3000m, 6 km / Trek)\n  The trail snakes steadily upward, emerging from dense tree cover to reveal sweeping views of the surrounding valleys. The rhythmic sound of rushing water accompanies your final approach to camp.\n  *Chikka*: A pristine campsite located right beside a vibrant mountain stream, offering phenomenal panoramic views.\n- Day 3 — Chikka to Seri (3600m, 5 km / Trek)\n  Ascend gradually through varied terrain, where the rocky path gives way to an impossibly vast, green expanse. The tree line fades, leaving you in a sprawling alpine pasture.\n  *Seri*: A magnificent, wide-open meadow offering front-row views of the Dhauladhar and Pir Panjal ranges—perfect for stargazing.\n- Day 4 — Seri to Deo Tibba Base Camp & back to Seri (4480m, 10 km / Trek)\n  The crescendo of the trek. You'll push up challenging, rocky paths and glacial moraines, drawing thrillingly close to the majestic ice dome of Deo Tibba. After soaking in the raw, freezing beauty of the base camp, you'll descend back to the comfort of the meadow.\n  *Seri*: Return to your familiar meadow camp, your muscles tired but your spirit soaring from the high-altitude views.\n- Day 5 — Seri to Jagatsukh (2050m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Begin the long, rewarding descent, retracing your steps through Chikka and Khanol. The gravity-assisted walk offers a new perspective on the lush, lower-elevation forests.\n  *Jagatsukh*: A historic gateway village famous for its ancient Shiva temple, marking the triumphant end of your foot journey.\n- Day 6 — Jagatsukh to Manali (2050m, 6 km / Drive)\n  Enjoy a hearty breakfast in the village before a brief, scenic drive back to the comforts of civilization.\n  *Manali*: The bustling hill station hub where you can enjoy a hot shower and celebrate your high-altitude achievement.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Deo Tibba Base Camp: An Engaging Himalayan Trek", "dhampus-australian-camp": "## Dhampus Australian Camp\n- **ID**: `dhampus-australian-camp`\n- **Location**: Annapurna, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 2 days | **Max Altitude**: 2060m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Escape the lakeside bustle of Pokhara for a tranquil weekend walk through terraced fields, culminating in a spectacular, unobstructed sunrise over the Annapurna and Machapuchare peaks.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Incredibly accessible weekend trek near Pokhara\n- Sweeping, golden-hour views of the Annapurna range\n- Beginner-friendly, gentle trails perfect for families\n- Authentic immersion in local Gurung village life\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Australian Camp base (820m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Drive the short distance from Pokhara to the trailhead, then ease into your trek with a gentle walk through lush, subtropical forests and stone-paved village paths.\n  *Australian Camp Base*: A bustling roadside gateway where you can grab a hot tea before beginning your short ascent.\n- Day 2 — Return to base (820m, 8 km + drive)\n  Wake up to a crisp mountain sunrise illuminating the peaks. After breakfast, descend through vibrant farming terraces, soaking in the village atmosphere before your drive back.\n  *Australian Camp Base*: The familiar starting point where your vehicle waits to return you to Pokhara's lakeside cafes.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Dhampus Australian Camp: A Quick Himalayan Escape", "dhorpatan": "## Dhorpatan Hunting Reserve Trek\n- **ID**: `dhorpatan`\n- **Location**: Dhaulagiri, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 8 days | **Max Altitude**: 3200m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Trek into Nepal's sole hunting reserve, a wild and remote expanse where elusive blue sheep roam beneath the colossal, icy gaze of the Dhaulagiri range.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Access to Nepal's only officially designated hunting reserve\n- High chances of spotting the elusive Blue Sheep and other rare wildlife\n- Phenomenal, off-the-beaten-path views of the Dhaulagiri massif\n- Deeply remote trails passing through isolated, traditional villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Dhorpatan base (1200m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Following a rugged drive into western Nepal, you take your first steps onto the trail, leaving the noise of the lowlands behind for the crisp air of the foothills.\n  *Dhorpatan Base*: The rustic gateway settlement where you prep your gear for the wild days ahead.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (2200m, 12 km)\n  A long, steady climb through terraced farmlands and dense, fragrant pine forests. The sounds of civilization are quickly replaced by bird calls and rushing water.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet clearing at 2200m, offering your first true night in the remote wilderness.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (2700m, 8 km)\n  The forest begins to thin as you push higher into alpine territory. The massive, snow-capped peaks of the Dhaulagiri range begin to peek over the horizon.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 2700m, where the evening air carries a sharp, refreshing alpine chill.\n- Day 4 — Summit / High Point (3200m, 6 km)\n  You break through the tree line into sweeping alpine pastures. This is the prime habitat for the famous Blue Sheep. You stand at 3200m, gazing at the panoramic sweep of the Himalayas.\n  *Dhorpatan High Camp*: The apex of your trek. An exposed, wildly beautiful vantage point with sweeping views of Dhaulagiri.\n- Day 5 — Descend to Camp 3 (2700m, 6 km)\n  Begin your descent back into the thicker air. Keep your eyes peeled for wildlife moving through the brush as you re-enter the upper forests.\n  *Camp 3*: Return to your 2700m campsite for a warm meal and well-earned rest.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 2 (2200m, 6 km)\n  The trail drops quickly through the pine and rhododendron forests, offering a completely different visual perspective on the way down.\n  *Camp 2*: Enjoy the thicker, warmer air of the 2200m elevation.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 1 (1700m, 9 km)\n  Pass back through isolated farming communities, witnessing the timeless agricultural lifestyle of Nepal's remote middle hills.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail, camped peacefully at 1700m.\n- Day 8 — Return to base (1200m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final walk brings you back to the roadhead. Board your vehicle for the bumpy, scenic drive back to Pokhara or Kathmandu.\n  *Dhorpatan Base*: The gateway village where you bid farewell to your trekking crew and celebrate your journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Dhorpatan Hunting Reserve: Into the Wild", "everest-panorama": "## Everest Panorama\n- **ID**: `everest-panorama`\n- **Location**: Khumbu, Solukhumbu, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 3880m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Experience the spiritual and visual grandeur of the Khumbu without the grueling high-altitude commitment, tracing a culturally rich path to the iconic Tengboche Monastery.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Breathtaking views of Everest, Lhotse, and Ama Dablam\n- A shorter, highly rewarding alternative to the full EBC trek\n- Exploring the spiritual heart of the Khumbu at Tengboche Monastery\n- Deep immersion into authentic Sherpa culture and hospitality\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Everest Panorama base (2610m, Drive + 3 km)\n  After the dramatic flight to Lukla, begin your trek with a gentle descent into the Dudh Kosi valley, passing beautifully painted mani stones.\n  *Everest Panorama Base*: The gateway of Lukla/Phakding, teeming with excitement and fellow trekkers.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3245m, 7 km)\n  Cross the thrilling Hillary Suspension Bridge before tackling the steep, pine-scented zigzag climb up to the bustling Sherpa capital.\n  *Camp 2*: Namche Bazaar, where you can enjoy fresh pastries while getting your first tantalizing glimpse of Everest.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3563m, 6 km)\n  A day of active acclimatization. Hike up to the Everest View Hotel for stunning panoramas, returning to lower elevations to sleep.\n  *Camp 3*: Khumjung or similar nearby village, characterized by its green roofs and quiet, traditional Sherpa atmosphere.\n- Day 4 — Summit / High Point (3880m, 6 km)\n  Descend to the river before a long, dusty climb through rhododendron forests brings you to the spiritual climax of the trek. As you crest the ridge, the Everest massif reveals itself in all its glory behind the legendary monastery.\n  *Everest Panorama High Camp*: Tengboche (3880m), home to the largest gompa in the Khumbu and surrounded by a 360-degree Himalayan panorama.\n- Day 5 — Descend to Camp 2 (3457m, 9 km)\n  Wake up for the morning prayers at the monastery, then begin the trek back, plunging down to the river and climbing back up the valley walls.\n  *Camp 2*: Return to the lively, welcoming comforts of Namche Bazaar.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 1 (3033m, 10 km)\n  The final long descent out of the national park, tracing the milky blue waters of the Dudh Kosi back toward civilization.\n  *Camp 1*: A riverside teahouse stop, enjoying the dense, oxygen-rich air of the lower valleys.\n- Day 7 — Return to base (2610m, 8 km + drive)\n  A short, rolling walk back to the airstrip. Spend the evening celebrating with your guides and porters.\n  *Everest Panorama Base*: Lukla, where you await your morning flight back to Kathmandu.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Everest Panorama: The Short Path to the Giants", "ganesh-himal": "## Ganesh Himal\n- **ID**: `ganesh-himal`\n- **Location**: Ganesh Himal, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 8 days | **Max Altitude**: 3800m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Lose yourself in the pristine, terraced valleys of the Ganesh Himal, where welcoming Tamang and Gurung communities share their heritage far from the crowded commercial trails.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Spectacular, close-up views of the gleaming Ganesh Himal range\n- Deep cultural immersion in traditional Tamang and Gurung villages\n- A truly off-the-beaten-path route with very few other trekkers\n- Staying in community-run lodges that directly benefit local residents\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Ganesh Himal base (800m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Leave the Kathmandu Valley behind on a scenic, bumpy drive, arriving at the lush, low-altitude trailhead to begin your walk into the hills.\n  *Ganesh Himal Base*: A vibrant farming village acting as your entry point into the rural heartland of central Nepal.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (2300m, 7 km)\n  The trail climbs steeply through tiered agricultural terraces and sub-tropical forests, with the air cooling noticeably as you gain elevation.\n  *Camp 2*: A traditional village homestay at 2300m, offering your first taste of authentic Tamang hospitality.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3050m, 5 km)\n  Navigate through dense rhododendron and oak forests. The tree canopy breaks occasionally to offer your first stunning glimpses of the snow-capped Ganesh peaks.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up at 3050m, where the dense forest gives way to the lower edges of the alpine pastures.\n- Day 4 — Summit / High Point (3800m, 6 km)\n  A breathtaking hike up to the trek's highest ridge. You are rewarded with a vast, unobstructed panorama of the Ganesh Himal massif, named after the elephant-headed Hindu deity.\n  *Ganesh Himal High Camp*: An exposed, spectacularly scenic viewpoint at 3800m, dominating the surrounding valleys.\n- Day 5 — Descend to Camp 3 (3050m, 10 km)\n  Begin your descent, tracing the ridge line before dropping back into the sheltering embrace of the mountain forests.\n  *Camp 3*: Return to 3050m for a restful evening filled with hot tea and hearty local food.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 2 (2300m, 10 km)\n  The descent is long but gentle on the knees, passing through pastures where locals graze their sheep and goats.\n  *Camp 2*: A comfortable return to the 2300m village, perhaps joining the locals in a traditional evening dance.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 1 (1550m, 10 km)\n  The climate warms rapidly as you drop back into the vibrant, green lower valleys, walking alongside rushing river gorges.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail, surrounded by the lush flora of the 1550m elevation.\n- Day 8 — Return to base (800m, 8 km + drive)\n  Complete the final leg of your walk back to the roadhead, where your vehicle awaits to transport you back to the chaos of Kathmandu.\n  *Ganesh Himal Base*: The end of your trek, leaving you with memories of a profoundly authentic Himalayan journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Ganesh Himal: The Hidden Ruby Valley", "great-himalaya-trail": "## Great Himalaya Trail (GHT)\n- **ID**: `great-himalaya-trail`\n- **Location**: Cross-Nepal, Multi-Province, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 150 days | **Max Altitude**: 6146m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Embark on the ultimate 1,700-kilometer alpine odyssey, traversing the entire length of Nepal's rugged spine through remote wilderness and soaring 6,000-meter passes.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- An epic 1,700 km traverse across the entire length of Nepal\n- Committing to a 150+ day expedition-style mountaineering journey\n- Crossing brutal, glaciated passes like the 6,146m Sherpani Col\n- The ultimate life achievement for elite high-altitude adventurers\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at GHT Section 1 base (1500m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Travel to the eastern trailhead, taking a deep breath before stepping off the edge of the map for five months. Briefing and massive gear check.\n  *GHT Section 1 Base*: The starting point in far eastern Nepal, where the monumental journey begins.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3358m, 10 km)\n  The initial shock to the system as you leave civilization, climbing steeply through the lush, dense forests of the Kanchenjunga region.\n  *Camp 2*: A remote, wild campsite setting the tone for the months to come.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (4288m, 6 km)\n  Break through the tree line into the stark, oxygen-deprived alpine zone. The sheer scale of the Himalayas begins to reveal itself.\n  *Camp 3*: High-altitude camping where the cold becomes a constant companion.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (5217m, 10 km)\n  Transitioning from trekking to mountaineering terrain, navigating moraines and the edges of vast, ancient glaciers.\n  *Camp 4*: A brutal, freezing camp at 5217m, testing your physical and mental limits.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (6146m, 6 km)\n  The technical crux. Roped up with crampons and ice axes, you navigate crevasses to conquer passes like the terrifying Sherpani Col.\n  *GHT High Camp*: The absolute highest extreme of the trail at 6146m; a place for alpinists, not casual hikers.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 4 (5217m, 10 km)\n  The harrowing, technical descent requiring fixed ropes and immense concentration as you drop off the high cols.\n  *Camp 4*: Relief washes over you as you reach the safety of the 5217m camp.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 3 (4288m, 12 km)\n  A long day of losing altitude, transitioning from glacier back to solid rock and sparse alpine tundra.\n  *Camp 3*: The air feels miraculously thick at 4288m compared to the high passes.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 2 (3358m, 7 km)\n  Plunging back into the tree line, the smell of earth and vegetation returning as a sensory shock.\n  *Camp 2*: A warmer night spent among the pines and rhododendrons.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 1 (2429m, 5 km)\n  Walking through remote, isolated villages that rarely see outsiders, experiencing the raw, unfiltered culture of the high Himalayas.\n  *Camp 1*: A culturally rich campsite in the lower middle-hills.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (1500m, 8 km + drive)\n  *(Note: This represents a single 10-day section profile of the massive 150-day traverse)*. You reach a resupply town, ready to restock, patch your blisters, and push westward into the next region.\n  *GHT Section Base*: A logistical hub where expeditions pause to breathe before continuing the trans-Nepal traverse.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Great Himalaya Trail (GHT): The Ultimate Traverse", "guerrilla-trail": "## Guerrilla Trail\n- **ID**: `guerrilla-trail`\n- **Location**: Mid-West, Lumbini, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 12 days | **Max Altitude**: 3200m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Walk through the raw, unpolished heart of mid-western Nepal, retracing the historic paths of the Maoist insurgency through dense jungles and remote, welcoming villages.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trek a living piece of history along the former Maoist insurgency routes\n- Deep immersion in some of Nepal's most remote, untouristed regions\n- Authentic cultural encounters with the resilient Magar and Chhetri communities\n- Pristine, unexplored landscapes spanning deep valleys and high alpine ridges\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Guerrilla Trail base (600m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Following a long, rugged drive into Nepal's mid-west, you stretch your legs with a short introductory walk through the sultry, lowland heat.\n  *Guerrilla Trail Base*: A bustling regional hub, often Beni or a similar settlement, serving as the gateway to the remote hills.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (1467m, 10 km)\n  The trail begins its relentless upward winding. You leave the dusty roads behind, entering terraced farmlands and small hamlets where the rhythm of life remains unchanged by time.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet, traditional village at 1467m where you'll experience your first authentic local homestay.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (1900m, 6 km)\n  Climb deeper into the forested hillsides. The air begins to cool as you pass through historic waypoints once used as rebel hideouts during the civil war.\n  *Camp 3*: Nestled at 1900m, this camp offers a peaceful evening surrounded by dense, whispering forests.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (2333m, 6 km)\n  The path steepens, winding through thick rhododendron and oak forests. You'll cross roaring rivers via swinging suspension bridges, completely isolated from the modern world.\n  *Camp 4*: A remote clearing or high-altitude settlement at 2333m, offering spectacular views of the valleys below.\n- Day 5 — Ascend to Camp 5 (2767m, 10 km)\n  Emerge from the dense tree line into the sweeping sub-alpine zone. The landscape opens up, revealing distant, snow-capped Himalayan sentinels.\n  *Camp 5*: Set up camp at 2767m, where the air is crisp and the nights are vividly starlit.\n- Day 6 — Summit / High Point (3200m, 6 km)\n  Reach the crest of the trail. Standing on the high ridges at 3200m, you command panoramic views over the Dhaulagiri range and the sprawling, untouched valleys of the mid-west.\n  *Guerrilla Trail High Camp*: The exhilarating apex of the trek, offering profound silence and sweeping mountain vistas.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 5 (2767m, 8 km)\n  Begin your multi-day descent, trading the exposed, windy ridges for the shelter of the upper forests. \n  *Camp 5*: A familiar, welcoming campsite at 2767m to rest your knees.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 4 (2333m, 10 km)\n  Drop rapidly through the alpine scrub back into the thick, humid embrace of the oak and rhododendron forests.\n  *Camp 4*: Enjoy the thicker, oxygen-rich air at 2333m.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 3 (1900m, 12 km)\n  A long day of trekking through deeply traditional Magar villages, where locals may share stories of the region's turbulent yet fascinating recent history.\n  *Camp 3*: A warm, culturally immersive evening at 1900m.\n- Day 10 — Descend to Camp 2 (1467m, 8 km)\n  The landscape transitions back to cascading agricultural terraces as you approach the lower valleys.\n  *Camp 2*: Spend your evening watching farmers tend to their crops in the fading light.\n- Day 11 — Descend to Camp 1 (1033m, 6 km)\n  The heat of the lowlands returns. You follow the river valleys, walking along paths carved into the hillside over centuries.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final wilderness camp at 1033m, listening to the roar of the river nearby.\n- Day 12 — Return to base (600m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final hike brings you back to the dirt roads of the mid-west. Board your transport for the long drive back to Kathmandu or Pokhara.\n  *Guerrilla Trail Base*: Bid farewell to the remote hills and celebrate the completion of a truly unique journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Guerrilla Trail: Walking Through History", "island-peak": "## Island Peak (Imja Tse)\n- **ID**: `island-peak`\n- **Location**: Khumbu, Solukhumbu, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 18 days | **Max Altitude**: 6189m\n- **Best Months**: 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Transition from trekker to mountaineer as you cross crevasse-riddled glaciers and climb fixed ropes to the razor-sharp 6,189m summit of Island Peak, surrounded by the giants of the Khumbu.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Conquering a 6,189m Himalayan summit requiring actual mountaineering gear\n- Combines the classic Everest Base Camp acclimatization trek with a peak climb\n- Navigating a massive glacier and ascending a breathtaking, sheer 45-degree ice headwall\n- Summit views featuring Lhotse, Makalu, and Ama Dablam seemingly at arm's reach\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Island Peak base (2610m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Fly into the thrilling Lukla airstrip and begin your trek with a gentle walk along the Dudh Kosi river, feeling the buzz of the Khumbu valley.\n  *Island Peak Base*: Phakding, a lively river-side settlement where the epic journey begins.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3405m, 12 km)\n  Cross the high Hillary Suspension Bridge and push up the steep, pine-clad hill into the bustling Sherpa capital.\n  *Camp 2*: Namche Bazaar, where you will spend crucial time acclimatizing to the thinning air.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3803m, 11 km)\n  The trail rolls high above the river, offering spectacular views of Everest and Ama Dablam before dropping down and climbing back up to Tengboche.\n  *Camp 3*: Tengboche, home to the famous monastery where climbers seek blessings from the Lama.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (4201m, 6 km)\n  The tree line vanishes. You walk through stark, beautiful alpine scrubland, passing stone walls and grazing yaks as you approach Dingboche.\n  *Camp 4*: Dingboche, a sun-drenched valley settlement crucial for your second phase of acclimatization.\n- Day 5 — Ascend to Camp 5 (4598m, 12 km)\n  *(Typically includes an EBC acclimatization push)*. You hike across the moraine of the Khumbu Glacier, surrounded by memorials to fallen climbers.\n  *Camp 5*: Lobuche, a cold, rocky settlement sitting in the shadow of Nuptse.\n- Day 6 — Ascend to Camp 6 (4996m, 9 km)\n  Trek alongside the grinding Khumbu Glacier, often reaching Gorak Shep and making the push to Everest Base Camp before returning to sleep.\n  *Camp 6*: Gorak Shep, the final, freezing teahouse outpost before the high peaks.\n- Day 7 — Ascend to Camp 7 (5394m, 12 km)\n  Climb Kala Patthar for sunrise views of Everest, then veer off the main EBC trail, descending into the Chukhung Valley toward your objective.\n  *Camp 7*: Chukhung, the rugged staging post for the Island Peak expedition.\n- Day 8 — Ascend to Camp 8 (5791m, 10 km)\n  Leave the teahouses behind and hike up a steep, rocky moraine to establish your high camp. Here, your guides will conduct a final review of crampon and jumar techniques.\n  *Camp 8*: Island Peak High Camp. Sleep in tents on a rocky, exposed ridge, anticipating the alpine start.\n- Day 9 — Summit Island Peak (6189m, 6 km)\n  Wake at 1:00 AM. Scramble up a rocky gully, strap on crampons, and rope up for the glacier. The crux is a grueling 150-meter, 45-degree ice headwall requiring ascenders. Reach the razor-thin summit (6189m) as the sun ignites the Himalayas.\n  *Island Peak High Camp*: The exhilarating, knife-edge summit, offering arguably the best 360-degree view in the Everest region.\n- Day 10 — Descend to Camp 8 (5791m, 5 km)\n  After the summit, carefully rappel down the headwall and navigate the glacier back to High Camp to collapse into your tent.\n  *Camp 8*: Rest and rehydrate after a 10-14 hour summit day.\n- Day 11 — Descend to Camp 7 (5394m, 7 km)\n  Pack up High Camp and trek back down the moraine to the warmth of a teahouse.\n  *Camp 7*: Chukhung, where the air suddenly feels wonderfully thick.\n- Day 12 — Descend to Camp 6 (4996m, 9 km)\n  Begin the long walk out of the high alpine zone, feeling stronger with every downward step.\n  *Camp 6*: Lobuche (or Dingboche, depending on routing), returning to the main trail.\n- Day 13 — Descend to Camp 5 (4598m, 10 km)\n  A relatively easy descent through the wide, windswept valleys.\n  *Camp 5*: Pheriche or Dingboche.\n- Day 14 — Descend to Camp 4 (4201m, 6 km)\n  Drop back into the rhododendron zones.\n  *Camp 4*: Pangboche, home to the oldest monastery in the Khumbu.\n- Day 15 — Descend to Camp 3 (3803m, 5 km)\n  A short walk back up to the Tengboche ridge.\n  *Camp 3*: Tengboche, a great place to reflect on the summit.\n- Day 16 — Descend to Camp 2 (3405m, 5 km)\n  Plunge down to the river and endure the final uphill sting back into Namche.\n  *Camp 2*: Namche Bazaar. Time for a celebratory beer and a hot shower!\n- Day 17 — Descend to Camp 1 (3008m, 7 km)\n  The knees take the brunt today as you descend the steep Namche hill back into the lower valley.\n  *Camp 1*: Phakding, spending your final night on the trail.\n- Day 18 — Return to base (2610m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final hike into Lukla to catch your flight back to Kathmandu. \n  *Island Peak Base*: Lukla airstrip. The expedition is complete.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Island Peak (Imja Tse): The Bridge to Mountaineering", "jomsom-muktinath": "## Jomsom Muktinath\n- **ID**: `jomsom-muktinath`\n- **Location**: Annapurna, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 6 days | **Max Altitude**: 3800m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Walk through the world's deepest gorge into a high-altitude desert, tracing the Kali Gandaki river to the sacred, eternal flames of Muktinath.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trekking through the Kali Gandaki, the deepest river gorge in the world\n- Visiting Muktinath, an immensely sacred pilgrimage site for both Hindus and Buddhists\n- Wandering through the apple orchards and white-washed stone alleys of Marpha\n- Experiencing the stark, Tibetan-plateau-like landscapes of the Mustang region\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Jomsom Muktinath base (2720m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A dramatic flight between the Annapurna and Dhaulagiri peaks drops you in Jomsom. The fierce valley winds greet you as you begin a short walk to your first stop.\n  *Jomsom Muktinath Base*: Jomsom, the administrative hub of the Mustang district, famous for its intense afternoon winds and apple brandy.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3440m, 11 km)\n  Walk along the rocky, arid riverbed of the Kali Gandaki. The landscape is a stark, striking desert framed by 8,000-meter snow peaks, leading you into the ancient village of Kagbeni.\n  *Camp 2*: Kagbeni, a medieval-feeling town with tightly packed mud-brick houses, serving as the gateway to Upper Mustang.\n- Day 3 — Summit / High Point (3800m, 6 km)\n  A steep, relentless climb out of the river valley. The air thins as you ascend the barren, dusty hills to reach the temple complex, where 108 water spouts and eternal flames await.\n  *Jomsom Muktinath High Camp*: Muktinath (3800m), a deeply spiritual sanctuary bustling with Hindu and Buddhist pilgrims from across South Asia.\n- Day 4 — Descend to Camp 2 (3440m, 5 km)\n  After morning prayers, descend back towards the valley floor. The sweeping views of Dhaulagiri dominating the horizon make the downhill walk spectacular.\n  *Camp 2*: Kagbeni (or a nearby settlement), offering a warm refuge from the howling afternoon valley winds.\n- Day 5 — Descend to Camp 1 (3080m, 5 km)\n  Walk south along the river, arriving at one of the most picturesque villages in the Himalayas, famous for its apple orchards and flat-roofed stone architecture.\n  *Camp 1*: Marpha, a charming Thakali village where you can indulge in fresh apple pie and local cider.\n- Day 6 — Return to base (2720m, 8 km + drive)\n  A final, easy walk back along the riverbed brings you full circle to the airstrip, concluding your high-desert pilgrimage.\n  *Jomsom Muktinath Base*: Return to Jomsom to catch your early morning flight back to Pokhara.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Jomsom Muktinath: The Sacred Desert Trek", "jugal-himal": "## Jugal Himal\n- **ID**: `jugal-himal`\n- **Location**: Langtang, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Difficult | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 4500m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Venture into the closest snow-capped mountains to Kathmandu, navigating dense, untamed forests to a pristine, glacial amphitheater near the Tibetan border.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trekking the mountain range closest to Nepal's capital, yet entirely off the commercial radar\n- Intimate, untouched wilderness camping near the Tibetan border\n- Phenomenal views of Dorje Lakpa and the unclimbed peaks of the Jugal massif\n- Exploring remote, traditional villages that see just a handful of trekkers a year\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Jugal Himal base (1400m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A bumpy drive from the Kathmandu Valley drops you at the trailhead. You immediately begin an ascent into the quiet, terraced foothills.\n  *Jugal Himal Base*: A traditional starting village, often Chautara, where the sounds of the city are immediately replaced by rural serenity.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (2640m, 8 km)\n  The path climbs aggressively through lush, subtropical forests. You leave the agricultural terraces behind, entering the domain of monkeys and dense undergrowth.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet forest clearing at 2640m, setting up your first wilderness camp.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3260m, 6 km)\n  Navigate through thick bamboo and rhododendron forests. The trail here is often narrow and wild, demanding your full attention.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 3260m, feeling the transition into the cooler sub-alpine zone.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (3880m, 10 km)\n  Break through the tree line into the high pastures. The sweeping vistas of the Jugal Himal range finally reveal themselves, dominating the northern skyline.\n  *Camp 4*: An exposed, spectacular alpine camp at 3880m.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (4500m, 6 km)\n  The physical climax of the trek. You push up steep, rocky terrain to the base camp of the Jugal peaks. Stand surrounded by glacial ice and towering, jagged summits right on the border of Tibet.\n  *Jugal Himal High Camp*: The ultimate vantage point at 4500m, an amphitheater of rock and ice offering profound isolation.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 4 (3880m, 9 km)\n  Begin the long retreat, leaving the high ice behind and descending back into the rolling alpine pastures.\n  *Camp 4*: A familiar, warmer camp at 3880m.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 3 (3260m, 8 km)\n  Plunge back into the dense rhododendron forests. The thick air and the smell of pine needles are a welcome sensory shift.\n  *Camp 3*: Camp in the sheltered woods at 3260m.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 2 (2640m, 5 km)\n  Continue losing altitude rapidly. The trail is steep, testing your knees as you navigate the forest floor.\n  *Camp 2*: Enjoy the warmer, thicker air at 2640m.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 1 (2020m, 8 km)\n  Return to the realm of human habitation, passing small herder huts and the highest agricultural terraces.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night in a tent, camped peacefully at 2020m.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (1400m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final walk back to the roadhead. Board your vehicle for the short drive back to the chaotic, vibrant streets of Kathmandu.\n  *Jugal Himal Base*: The gateway village where your wilderness expedition concludes.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Jugal Himal: The Capital's Hidden Peaks", "karnali-corridor": "## Karnali Corridor\n- **ID**: `karnali-corridor`\n- **Location**: Karnali, Karnali, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 3500m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Trace the roaring course of the mighty Karnali River through deep, wild gorges, stepping into an isolated realm of western Nepal where pristine nature and ancient trade routes converge.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trek along the dramatic Karnali River gorge, Nepal’s longest river\n- Deeply off-the-beaten-path exploration of the rugged western wilderness\n- Unique opportunities to spot rare local wildlife in undisturbed habitats\n- Authentic cultural encounters in isolated, traditional middle-hill villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Karnali Corridor base (800m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Your expedition begins with a long, scenic journey into the remote west. You set foot on the trail with a gentle walk, acclimatizing to the humid, lower-elevation valley.\n  *Karnali Corridor Base*: A rustic gateway town in western Nepal, serving as the launching pad for deep wilderness exploration.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (1880m, 12 km)\n  The trail snakes upstream along the rocky river banks. You climb steadily as the valley walls begin to close in, revealing dramatic cliffs and dense subtropical vegetation.\n  *Camp 2*: A simple clearing at 1880m, where the air starts to cool and the sound of the roaring river dominates the night.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (2420m, 11 km)\n  Ascend through beautiful pine and oak forests, crossing suspension bridges suspended high above rushing tributaries. The trail feels raw and wild.\n  *Camp 3*: A quiet hillside settlement at 2420m, offering panoramic views of the rugged Karnali landscapes.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (2960m, 11 km)\n  The subtropical forests give way to crisp sub-alpine terrain. You navigate stone steps carved into the cliffs, watching for langurs and local birdlife.\n  *Camp 4*: A scenic camp at 2960m, surrounded by alpine shrubs and towering rocky peaks.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (3500m, 6 km)\n  The ultimate point of your river corridor journey. You climb to a spectacular high vantage point at 3500m, commanding sweeping views of the deep Karnali gorge and distant snow-capped western peaks.\n  *Karnali Corridor High Camp*: The scenic climax of the trek at 3500m, offering a stark, beautiful perspective of Nepal’s far-western frontier.\n- Day 6 — Descend to Camp 4 (2960m, 9 km)\n  Retrace your steps, leaving the thin air of the high ridges behind as you descend back into the sheltering valleys.\n  *Camp 4*: A restful evening at 2960m, enjoying the increased oxygen levels.\n- Day 7 — Descend to Camp 3 (2420m, 6 km)\n  Continue downward, passing through small herder settlements where locals live self-sufficiently, far from modern amenities.\n  *Camp 3*: A peaceful evening spent at 2420m in a welcoming local village environment.\n- Day 8 — Descend to Camp 2 (1880m, 9 km)\n  The forest canopy thickens as you drop back into the warm river valley. The familiar, rhythmic roar of the Karnali grows louder.\n  *Camp 2*: Relax at 1880m, enjoying the warmer temperatures.\n- Day 9 — Descend to Camp 1 (1340m, 8 km)\n  A leisurely walk down the valley floor, passing through vibrant farmlands and small rural trading posts.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final camp on the trail at 1340m, reflecting on the vast landscapes crossed.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (800m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final stretch brings you back to the roadhead. Board your vehicle for the rugged drive back to the regional airport or transport hub.\n  *Karnali Corridor Base*: Celebrate the successful completion of an incredibly remote western trek.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Karnali Corridor: The Wild West of Nepal", "kashmir-great-lakes": "## Kashmir Great Lakes\n- **ID**: `kashmir-great-lakes`\n- **Location**: Kashmir, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 4100m\n- **Best Months**: 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Ascend into an alpine wonderland where seven pristine, jewel-toned lakes lie cradled between towering mountain passes and vast, wildflower-carpeted meadows.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trekking past seven distinct, sapphire-colored alpine lakes\n- Crossing the spectacular and challenging Gadsar Pass at 4100m\n- Camping in vast, lush green meadows ringed by towering pine forests\n- Experiencing the raw, untamed beauty of the Pir Panjal wilderness\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Srinagar to Sonamarg & Trek to Shitkadi (2730m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Drive from the houseboats of Srinagar to the golden meadows of Sonamarg. Step onto the trail at Shitkadi, walking a short distance to your first alpine camp.\n  *Shitkadi*: A scenic, riverside meadow acting as the traditional starting campsite of the trek, surrounded by towering pines.\n- Day 2 — Shitkadi to Nichnai (3500m, 11 km / Trek)\n  The trail climbs steadily along the roaring Nichnai stream. You wind through dense pine forests and wide meadows, with the snow-capped peaks looming ahead.\n  *Nichnai*: A rugged, high-altitude campsite at 3500m, set in a deep valley beneath a massive mountain pass.\n- Day 3 — Nichnai to Vishansar Lake (3710m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Cross the Nichnai Pass (4080m) before descending into a wide, flower-filled valley. The day culminates at the breathtaking, deep-blue shores of Vishansar Lake.\n  *Vishansar Lake*: A massive, pristine alpine lake at 3710m, sacred to locals and teeming with wild trout.\n- Day 4 — Vishansar to Gadsar Lake via Gadsar Pass (4100m, 14 km / Trek)\n  Trek past the twin Kishansar Lake before tackling the steep, challenging climb to Gadsar Pass (4100m), the highest point of the trek. Descend into a valley of untouched lakes.\n  *Gadsar Lake*: A quiet, emerald-green lake nestled in a dramatic, rocky basin—frequently described as the most pristine on the trail.\n- Day 5 — Gadsar to Satsar Lake (3650m, 9 km / Trek)\n  Climb out of the Gadsar valley, crossing rolling green ridges and passing basic shepherd encampments. Reach Satsar, a collection of seven small, interconnected tarns.\n  *Satsar*: A peaceful, high-altitude alpine meadow at 3650m, offering a serene campsite near the glacial lakes.\n- Day 6 — Satsar to Gangabal Twin Lakes (3580m, 11 km / Trek)\n  Cross the challenging, boulder-strewn Zajibal Pass (4000m). From the crest, the twin sapphire lakes of Gangabal and Nundkol erupt into view beneath the colossal wall of Mount Haramukh.\n  *Gangabal*: A spectacular twin-lake basin at 3580m, reflecting the sheer glaciers of Mount Haramukh.\n- Day 7 — Gangabal to Naranag & Drive to Srinagar (2250m, 13 km / Trek + Drive)\n  The final long descent. You plunge down a steep, pine-forested ridge, dropping rapidly to the ancient temple ruins of Naranag, where your vehicle waits.\n  *Naranag*: An ancient, historic village marked by 8th-century stone temple ruins, signaling the triumphant end of your trek.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Kashmir Great Lakes: The Alpine Masterpiece", "kopra-danda": "## Kopra Danda\n- **ID**: `kopra-danda`\n- **Location**: Annapurna, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 5 days | **Max Altitude**: 3660m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Ascend a quiet, dramatic ridge trail that sits directly opposite the colossal wall of Dhaulagiri and Annapurna South, bypassing the crowds for a peaceful mountain sanctuary.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Panoramic, front-row views of Dhaulagiri and Annapurna South\n- Quieter, pristine alternative to the crowded Poon Hill trails\n- Staying in community-run lodges that directly fund local village schools\n- Optional day hike to the sacred, high-altitude Khayer Lake (4600m)\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Kopra Danda base (1000m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Drive from Pokhara to the trailhead, launching your trek with a gentle climb through terraced farmlands and welcoming Gurung villages.\n  *Kopra Danda Base*: A charming middle-hills settlement, acting as your entry point into the quiet Annapurna ridges.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (2773m, 12 km)\n  A steady climb up through dense forests of oak and rhododendron. The trail is peaceful and remarkably quiet compared to the mainstream routes.\n  *Camp 2*: A cozy forest clearing at 2773m, offering a tranquil evening surrounded by nature.\n- Day 3 — Summit / High Point (3660m, 6 km)\n  The tree line thins rapidly as you push up the steep ridge. Cresting the spine of Kopra Danda, the massive, icy face of Dhaulagiri erupts directly across the valley.\n  *Kopra Danda High Camp*: The jaw-dropping ridge outpost at 3660m, offering one of the most dramatic mountain views in Nepal.\n- Day 4 — Descend to Camp 1 (2330m, 8 km)\n  After a memorable sunrise over the peaks, begin a steady descent down the steep ridge line, passing through alpine pastures and back into the oak forests.\n  *Camp 1*: A comfortable forest clearing or small village lodge at 2330m, enjoying the thicker air.\n- Day 5 — Return to base (1000m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final downhill stretch brings you back to the valley floor, passing agricultural terraces before catching your vehicle back to Pokhara.\n  *Kopra Danda Base*: Return to the roadhead. Celebrate your successful, crowd-free Annapurna ridge adventure!\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Kopra Danda: The Great Annapurna Ridge", "limi-valley": "## Limi Valley\n- **ID**: `limi-valley`\n- **Location**: Far West, Sudurpashchim, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 18 days | **Max Altitude**: 5100m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Embark on an extraordinary expedition into Nepal's forgotten northwest, traversing high trans-Himalayan passes to a dry, wind-sculpted valley steeped in ancient Tibetan Bhotia culture.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- True trans-Himalayan expedition through Nepal's most remote northwest valley\n- Walking along ancient salt trading routes connecting Nepal and Tibet\n- Deep immersion in the ancient, untouched Bhotia Buddhist culture\n- Pristine, stark desert landscapes framed by high-altitude passes\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Limi Valley base (2500m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Fly into the remote mountain airstrip of Simikot. Step onto the trail, immediately entering a wild, isolated world.\n  *Limi Valley Base*: Simikot, the administrative capital of Humla, sitting on a high ridge completely disconnected from Nepal's road network.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (3078m, 6 km)\n  The trail snakes along the roaring Humla Karnali River. You walk beneath massive cliffs, passing pine forests and small Buddhist villages.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet, riverside clearing at 3078m, offering a peaceful night under the stars.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (3367m, 12 km)\n  A long day of steady climbing. The landscape begins to transition from green valleys to arid, wind-sculpted rock formations.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 3367m, surrounded by the stark beauty of the upper valley.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (3656m, 12 km)\n  Trek past ancient stone chortens and prayer walls. The altitude begins to make itself felt as you push deeper into the high borderlands.\n  *Camp 4*: A remote alpine campsite at 3656m, where the air is crisp and cold.\n- Day 5 — Ascend to Camp 5 (3944m, 7 km)\n  A shorter day to aid acclimatization. The trail rolls through barren pastures where nomadic herders graze their yaks.\n  *Camp 5*: Camp at 3944m, enjoying the vast, silent trans-Himalayan landscape.\n- Day 6 — Ascend to Camp 6 (4233m, 6 km)\n  Climb steadily into the high alpine desert. The vegetation is now sparse, consisting only of hardy mountain shrubs.\n  *Camp 6*: A rocky high camp at 4233m, acting as a critical staging ground for the high passes.\n- Day 7 — Ascend to Camp 7 (4522m, 12 km)\n  A long, demanding climb across moraines. The scale of the surrounding mountains is immense and humbling.\n  *Camp 7*: Establish your camp at 4522m, tucked beneath the towering, icy ridges.\n- Day 8 — Ascend to Camp 8 (4811m, 9 km)\n  Slowly wind your way up to your final high camp. The thin air demands a slow, rhythmic pace.\n  *Camp 8*: A freezing, high-altitude camp at 4811m, surrounded by glaciers and rock.\n- Day 9 — Summit / High Point (5100m, 6 km)\n  The ultimate goal. Cross the formidable Nyalu La pass (5000m+) or reach the 5100m high point, entering the secret, dry bowl of the Limi Valley.\n  *Limi Valley High Camp*: The jaw-dropping summit vantage point at 5100m, offering sweeping views into Tibet.\n- Day 10 — Descend to Camp 8 (4811m, 6 km)\n  Begin your return, carefully navigating the high pass scree fields back down to the glaciated valleys.\n  *Camp 8*: A restful return to the high camp, celebrating the pass crossing.\n- Day 11 — Descend to Camp 7 (4522m, 7 km)\n  Drop steadily back down the glacial valley, feeling the air grow warmer and richer with every kilometer.\n  *Camp 7*: Camp at 4522m, surrounded by stark mountain scenery.\n- Day 12 — Descend to Camp 6 (4233m, 8 km)\n  Retrace your steps through the rocky, dry terrain of the upper desert.\n  *Camp 6*: Enjoy a comfortable night at 4233m.\n- Day 13 — Descend to Camp 5 (3944m, 6 km)\n  A gentle descent back to the high grazing pastures.\n  *Camp 5*: Relax at 3944m, watching the evening light illuminate the hills.\n- Day 14 — Descend to Camp 4 (3656m, 12 km)\n  Continue descending, passing through ancient Bhotia villages like Halji and Jang, home to thousand-year-old monasteries.\n  *Camp 4*: A culturally rich stop at 3656m, exploring the timeless village life.\n- Day 15 — Descend to Camp 3 (3367m, 12 km)\n  Drop back into the upper tree line, where the sight of greenery is a welcome change.\n  *Camp 3*: Camp in the lower valley at 3367m.\n- Day 16 — Descend to Camp 2 (3078m, 5 km)\n  An easy day of walking along the river, enjoying the rich oxygen levels.\n  *Camp 2*: Spend a peaceful evening at 3078m.\n- Day 17 — Descend to Camp 1 (2789m, 5 km)\n  Approach the end of the wilderness, passing rural middle-hill farms.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail at 2789m.\n- Day 18 — Return to base (2500m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final hike brings you back to Simikot to catch your flight back to Nepalgunj.\n  *Limi Valley Base*: Simikot. Your extraordinary, 18-day trans-Himalayan journey is complete.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Limi Valley: Nepal's Forbidden Northwest", "lumba-sumba": "## Lumba Sumba Pass\n- **ID**: `lumba-sumba`\n- **Location**: Kanchenjunga, Province No. 1, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 20 days | **Max Altitude**: 5160m\n- **Best Months**: 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Embark on an epic, trans-regional expedition that scales a formidable 5,160m pass, linking the remote wilderness of Kanchenjunga with the dramatic valleys of Makalu.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- An epic crossover linking two of Nepal's most legendary eastern giants\n- Scaling the remote and wildly beautiful Lumba Sumba Pass at 5,160m\n- Exploring untouched, deep wilderness far from commercial tea trails\n- Experiencing the unique cultural transition from Limbu to Sherpa villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Lumba Sumba base (1500m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Your journey begins with a long overland drive into far eastern Nepal. Step onto the trail, leaving the humid lowlands for the mountain foothills.\n  *Lumba Sumba Base*: A remote starting village in the eastern hills, acting as the gateway to the high passes.\n- Day 2 — Ascend to Camp 2 (2232m, 9 km)\n  Climb through lush, subtropical forests and terraced cardamom fields, following roaring rivers upstream.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet forest clearing at 2232m, where the air begins to cool.\n- Day 3 — Ascend to Camp 3 (2598m, 8 km)\n  The trail winds through dense bamboo and rhododendron forests, crossing high suspension bridges.\n  *Camp 3*: A peaceful forest camp or small village settlement at 2598m.\n- Day 4 — Ascend to Camp 4 (2964m, 6 km)\n  The forest canopy begins to thin. You walk through mossy oak forests, passing small herder huts.\n  *Camp 4*: A scenic, secluded clearing at 2964m, surrounded by wilderness.\n- Day 5 — Ascend to Camp 5 (3330m, 7 km)\n  The air grows crisper as you enter the sub-alpine zone. The trail is wild and unmanicured, demanding careful foot placement.\n  *Camp 5*: Set up camp at 3330m, feeling the proximity to the high peaks.\n- Day 6 — Ascend to Camp 6 (3696m, 9 km)\n  Break through the tree line into vast, open alpine meadows, passing stone sheep-pens.\n  *Camp 6*: A spectacular high pasture camp at 3696m, offering panoramic valley views.\n- Day 7 — Ascend to Camp 7 (4062m, 10 km)\n  The trail turns rugged and rocky. You climb steadily along glacial moraines, entering the high-altitude desert.\n  *Camp 7*: An exposed campsite at 4062m, where the nights are freezing and clear.\n- Day 8 — Ascend to Camp 8 (4428m, 8 km)\n  Move slowly, pacing your breathing in the thin air. The massive peaks of the eastern Himalayas begin to dominate the horizon.\n  *Camp 8*: Set up camp at 4428m, surrounded by stark rock and ice.\n- Day 9 — Ascend to Camp 9 (4794m, 12 km)\n  A demanding, high-altitude push to position yourself for the pass. You camp on a glacial moraine.\n  *Camp 9*: The final high camp at 4794m, a freezing, awe-inspiring launchpad.\n- Day 10 — Summit Lumba Sumba Pass (5160m, 6 km)\n  The climax of the expedition. A pre-dawn start takes you up steep, snow-covered slopes to crest the 5,160m Lumba Sumba Pass, offering mind-bending views of Kanchenjunga and Makalu before a long descent.\n  *Lumba Sumba High Camp*: The windswept, high pass saddle at 5160m, a monumental physical achievement.\n- Day 11 — Descend to Camp 9 (4794m, 12 km)\n  Carefully descend the steep scree fields on the western side of the pass, returning to the high moraines.\n  *Camp 9*: A restful high-altitude camp to recover from the pass crossing.\n- Day 12 — Descend to Camp 8 (4428m, 6 km)\n  Continue losing altitude, the rocky moraine slowly giving way to sparse alpine scrub.\n  *Camp 8*: A welcome return to the 4428m elevation.\n- Day 13 — Descend to Camp 7 (4062m, 10 km)\n  Descend back into the upper valleys, where the air feels noticeably richer in oxygen.\n  *Camp 7*: Enjoy a peaceful, warmer night at 4062m.\n- Day 14 — Descend to Camp 6 (3696m, 7 km)\n  Re-enter the alpine meadows, passing local herders grazing their yaks.\n  *Camp 6*: A comfortable pasture camp at 3696m.\n- Day 15 — Descend to Camp 5 (3330m, 5 km)\n  The trail drops back into the upper forest canopy. The smell of damp earth and pine is rejuvenating.\n  *Camp 5*: Spend a peaceful evening at 3330m.\n- Day 16 — Descend to Camp 4 (2964m, 11 km)\n  Drop rapidly through the oak and bamboo zones, crossing roaring mountain streams.\n  *Camp 4*: A comfortable forest clearing camp at 2964m.\n- Day 17 — Descend to Camp 3 (2598m, 12 km)\n  Pass back through isolated farming communities, witnessing the traditional lifestyle of eastern Nepal.\n  *Camp 3*: A culturally warm homestay or campsite at 2598m.\n- Day 18 — Descend to Camp 2 (2232m, 10 km)\n  The climate warms rapidly as you reach the lower, lush agricultural valleys.\n  *Camp 2*: A comfortable return to the 2232m elevation.\n- Day 19 — Descend to Camp 1 (1866m, 6 km)\n  Walk through vibrant terraced farmlands, interacting with the welcoming local villagers.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail at 1866m.\n- Day 20 — Return to base (1500m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final hike brings you to the roadhead, where your vehicle waits to transport you back to Biratnagar or Tumlingtar.\n  *Lumba Sumba Base*: The end of your epic, 20-day crossover journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Lumba Sumba Pass: The Great Eastern Crossover", "markha-valley": "## Markha Valley\n- **ID**: `markha-valley`\n- **Location**: Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 5200m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Journey through Ladakh's iconic high-altitude desert valley, crossing the formidable Kongmaru La Pass and staying in traditional mud-brick homestays beneath towering Buddhist monasteries.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trekking through Ladakh's iconic, wind-sculpted high-altitude desert canyons\n- Climbing the spectacular and challenging Kongmaru La Pass at 5200m\n- Staying in authentic, whitewashed Ladakhi village homestays\n- Exploring ancient Buddhist monasteries perched precariously on cliffs\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive Leh to Chilling & Trek to Skiu (3400m, Drive 2 hrs, Trek 6 km)\n  Drive along the roaring Indus River to the trailhead at Chilling. Step onto the trail, immediately entering the dramatic, deep canyons of the Markha River, walking a short distance to Skiu.\n  *Skiu*: A tranquil, ancient village marked by a small Buddhist shrine and traditional mud-brick homes.\n- Day 2 — Skiu to Markha (3700m, 20 km / Trek)\n  A long but gentle walk through the heart of the canyon. The trail crosses the Markha stream multiple times, framed by towering, pink-and-purple rock formations.\n  *Markha*: The largest village in the valley, featuring a ruined fort and a spectacularly perched monastery.\n- Day 3 — Markha to Hankar (4000m, 11 km / Trek)\n  The valley opens up, revealing stunning views of Mount Kang Yatse. You pass small, green barley fields irrigated by glacial meltwater.\n  *Hankar*: A medieval-feeling village sitting beneath a ruined stone castle perched high on a rock spire.\n- Day 4 — Hankar to Nimaling (4700m, 10 km / Trek)\n  The trail climbs steeply out of the valley onto a vast, high-altitude alpine plateau. Nomadic herders graze their yaks and sheep beneath the glaciers of Kang Yatse.\n  *Nimaling*: A cold, sweeping pasture at 4700m, offering spectacular, close-up views of the snowy peaks.\n- Day 5 — Nimaling to Kongmaru La Pass & Shang Sumdo (5200m Pass, 12 km / Trek)\n  The physical climax. A steep, lung-busting climb brings you to the crest of the Kongmaru La Pass (5200m), offering views of the Karakoram before a long descent through a tight canyon.\n  *Shang Sumdo*: A welcoming village on the northern side of the pass, marking your return to lower elevations.\n- Day 6 — Shang Sumdo to Hemis (3600m, 8 km / Trek)\n  A gentle, downhill walk along the riverbed, bringing you to the gates of one of Ladakh's largest and most famous monasteries.\n  *Hemis*: A massive, wealthy Buddhist monastery dating back to the 17th century, famous for its annual festival.\n- Day 7 — Drive to Leh (3500m, Drive 1.5 hrs)\n  Board your vehicle for the scenic drive back to the comforts of Leh, reflecting on the spectacular high-altitude loop.\n  *Leh*: The bustling capital of Ladakh, where a hot shower and celebratory meal await.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Markha Valley: The Heart of Ladakh", "mustang-via-ferrata": "## Mustang Cave Trail\n- **ID**: `mustang-via-ferrata`\n- **Location**: Mustang, Gandaki, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 6 days | **Max Altitude**: 3200m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Explore the enigmatic, ancient sky caves and cliff dwellings carved vertically into the striking, wind-sculpted ochre cliffs of the forbidden Mustang region.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Exploring mysterious, multi-story sky caves dating back thousands of years\n- Trekking through a dramatic, high-altitude desert landscape of pastel canyons\n- Deep immersion in the preserved, ancient Tibetan-influenced Mustang culture\n- Visiting rare, active archaeological and cave-dwelling excavation sites\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Mustang Caves base (2700m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A scenic flight or rugged jeep drive brings you to the windswept valley floor. Take a brief introductory walk, feeling the dry, desert air on your skin.\n  *Mustang Caves Base*: Jomsom or Kagbeni, the historic medieval starting villages serving as gateways to the dry northern canyons.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (3033m, 9 km)\n  Walk alongside the dusty Kali Gandaki riverbed before climbing steadily up canyon trails where towering mud-brick cliffs are peppered with mysterious, vertical openings.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet, high-desert village campsite at 3033m, surrounded by wind-sculpted rock towers.\n- Day 3 — Summit / High Point (3200m, 6 km)\n  The physical and cultural climax. Scramble up the dusty trails to stand inside ancient sky caves carved into sheer cliffs, gazing over the vast, red-rock landscape.\n  *Mustang Caves High Camp*: The ultimate vantage point at 3200m, offering panoramic views of the spectacular, eroded canyons and distant snow peaks.\n- Day 4 — Day 4 - Descend (3033m, 5 km)\n  Carefully descend the steep, dry pathways from the high caves, returning to the lower canyon floors.\n  *Camp 2*: Return to the comfortable, wind-sheltered oasis of the 3033m village.\n- Day 5 — Day 5 - Descend (2867m, 7 km)\n  Continue downward, passing agricultural oases where locals cultivate barley using ancient irrigation systems.\n  *Camp 1*: A peaceful village camp or rustic lodge at 2867m, enjoying the warmer air.\n- Day 6 — Return to base (2700m, 8 km + drive)\n  Complete the final loop back to the main town to catch your vehicle or flight out.\n  *Mustang Caves Base*: Jomsom, where you celebrate your journey into the ancient archaeology of the sky caves.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Mustang Cave Trail: Secrets of the Sky Caves", "nagarkot-chisapani": "## Nagarkot to Chisapani\n- **ID**: `nagarkot-chisapani`\n- **Location**: Kathmandu Valley, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 2 days | **Max Altitude**: 2175m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Walk along the forested northern rim of the Kathmandu Valley, tracing a panoramic ridge that serves up stunning, uninterrupted views of the central Himalayan range.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Panoramic sunrise views of the Langtang and Everest ranges from Nagarkot\n- Gentle, forest trails passing through Shivapuri Nagarjun National Park\n- A perfect, low-altitude weekend trek starting right from Kathmandu\n- Immersive walks through tranquil Tamang farming communities\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Nagarkot base (1400m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A short, scenic drive from Kathmandu brings you to the hill station. You begin with a gentle walk along the ridge as the afternoon mist rolls through the pine forests.\n  *Nagarkot Base*: A famous viewpoint town perched at 1400m on the valley rim, lined with cozy hotels and viewing towers.\n- Day 2 — Return to base (1400m, 8 km + drive)\n  Wake up to a spectacular, golden sunrise over the snow-capped Himalayas. Hike along the ridge line through the lush national park forests to Chisapani, before descending to the roadhead.\n  *Nagarkot Base*: Return to the starting base or conclude at Sundarijal for the short drive back to Kathmandu.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Nagarkot to Chisapani: The Valley Rim Ridge", "naranag-mahlish": "## Naranag Mahlish Lake\n- **ID**: `naranag-mahlish`\n- **Location**: Kashmir, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 3 days | **Max Altitude**: 3600m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Discover a pristine, emerald-green alpine lake cradled in a high Kashmiri basin, starting your journey at the mystical, 8th-century stone temple ruins of Naranag.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Exploring the ancient, 8th-century Naranag temple ruins before the trek\n- Hiking through dense, fragrant pine and fir forests along the Wangath River\n- Camping in the quiet, isolated shepherd meadows of Dumail\n- Reaching the tranquil, emerald waters of Mahlish Lake at 3400m\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Naranag to Dumail: Following the Ancient Path (2400m, 6 km)\n  Explore the historic 8th-century stone temple ruins of Naranag before setting foot on the trail. You climb steadily through dense pine forests, accompanied by the roar of the Wangath River.\n  *Dumail*: A stunning, emerald-green meadow flanked by whispering pine forests, serving as a peaceful shepherd's outpost.\n- Day 2 — Dumail to Mahlish Lake: Ascending to the Emerald Gem (3400m, 9 km)\n  A steep, rewarding climb. The dense forest gives way to sprawling, high-altitude pastures where local Gujjar shepherds graze their sheep. Reach the pristine, glacial waters of the lake.\n  *Mahlish Lake Camp*: A serene, high-altitude camp situated directly beside the emerald waters, offering unparalleled stargazing.\n- Day 3 — Mahlish Lake to Naranag: The Descent Back to History (2128m, 15 km)\n  Wake to the crisp mountain air. After breakfast, begin a long, steady descent back down through the pine-forested ridges, retracing your steps to the Naranag ruins.\n  *Naranag*: The ancient stone temple complex marking your return to civilization, where your vehicle waits for the drive back to Srinagar.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Naranag Mahlish Lake: Kashmir's Hidden Gem", "numbur-cheese-circuit": "## Numbur Cheese Circuit\n- **ID**: `numbur-cheese-circuit`\n- **Location**: Khumbu, Solukhumbu, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 4636m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Venture off the beaten track on a unique, culturally rich circuit beneath the icy dome of Mount Numbur, visiting historic yak cheese factories and remote Sherpa monasteries.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Visiting Nepal’s oldest and most historic yak cheese factories\n- Phenomenal, close-up views of Mount Numbur (Shorong Yul Lha), the protector peak\n- Trekking through pristine, ancient forests of rhododendron and blue pine\n- Staying in authentic, untouched Sherpa and Shingo villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Numbur Circuit base (2000m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A scenic drive from Kathmandu brings you to the starting point. Step onto the trail, breathing in the fresh air of the lower Solukhumbu.\n  *Numbur Circuit Base*: Shivalaya or Bhandar, the historic starting gateway villages of the circuit.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (3054m, 11 km)\n  Climb steadily through lush terraced farmlands and ancient forests of oak and pine, with the air cooling as you gain altitude.\n  *Camp 2*: A traditional village campsite or rustic lodge at 3054m.\n- Day 3 — Day 3 - Ascend (3582m, 11 km)\n  The trail winds higher into sub-alpine territory, passing through beautiful pastures where local herders graze their massive yaks.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 3582m, feeling the crispness of the high altitude.\n- Day 4 — Day 4 - Ascend (4109m, 11 km)\n  Climb past stone mani walls and prayer flags. The imposing, icy pyramid of Mount Numbur begins to dominate the horizon.\n  *Camp 4*: An exposed, spectacular alpine campsite at 4109m.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (4636m, 6 km)\n  The climax of the circuit. Cross the formidable high pass or reach the 4636m high point, offering panoramic views of the Everest and Rolwaling ranges.\n  *Numbur Circuit High Camp*: The jaw-dropping high point at 4636m, offering spectacular views of Mount Numbur.\n- Day 6 — Day 6 - Descend (4109m, 7 km)\n  Begin your descent down the opposite side of the loop, navigating rocky trails back into the upper pastures.\n  *Camp 4*: A comfortable return to the 4109m elevation.\n- Day 7 — Day 7 - Descend (3582m, 5 km)\n  Descend back into the forest zone, where the sight of greenery and the smell of pine is a welcome sensory change.\n  *Camp 3*: A quiet, peaceful forest campsite at 3582m.\n- Day 8 — Day 8 - Descend (3054m, 5 km)\n  Pass through isolated Sherpa hamlets, perhaps stopping to taste fresh yak cheese straight from a local cooperative.\n  *Camp 2*: Enjoy a culturally warm evening in a local village at 3054m.\n- Day 9 — Day 9 - Descend (2527m, 5 km)\n  Continue descending through the terraced farmlands of the lower middle hills.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final camp on the trail at 2527m.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (2000m, 8 km + drive)\n  Complete the loop, returning to the roadhead for your vehicle transport back to Kathmandu.\n  *Numbur Circuit Base*: Celebrate the successful completion of a highly unique, off-the-beaten-path circuit.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Numbur Cheese Circuit: The Solukhumbu's Best Kept Secret", "panch-pokhari": "## Panch Pokhari\n- **ID**: `panch-pokhari`\n- **Location**: Langtang, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 4100m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Trek to five sacred, high-altitude alpine lakes tucked beneath the Jugal Himal, standing as one of Nepal's most revered Hindu and Buddhist pilgrimage sanctuaries.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Reaching the sacred \"Five Lakes\" (Panch Pokhari) at 4100m\n- Exploring an important, ancient Hindu and Buddhist pilgrimage sanctuary\n- Phenomenal, close-up views of the Jugal Himal and Dorje Lakpa peaks\n- Pristine, off-the-beaten-path trails passing through quiet Tamang villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Panch Pokhari base (1400m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A scenic drive from Kathmandu brings you to the starting trailhead. You begin your trek with a gentle walk, leaving the busy valleys behind.\n  *Panch Pokhari Base*: Chautara or Melamchi, the quiet starting gateway villages tucked into the green foothills.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (2750m, 10 km)\n  The trail climbs steadily up a prominent ridge line, winding through dense bamboo and pine forests.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet, forested ridge campsite at 2750m, offering a peaceful evening.\n- Day 3 — Day 3 - Ascend (3425m, 9 km)\n  Continue ascending along the ridge. The forest canopy breaks occasionally to offer spectacular glimpses of the snow-capped Jugal Himal.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 3425m, feeling the transition into the cool alpine zone.\n- Day 4 — Summit / High Point (4100m, 6 km)\n  The final climb brings you onto a high, open plateau. You emerge to see the five spectacular, mirror-like glacial lakes sitting in a silent mountain bowl.\n  *Panch Pokhari High Camp*: The sacred lakeside sanctuary at 4100m, a place of immense spiritual power and beauty.\n- Day 5 — Day 5 - Descend (3200m, 9 km)\n  After witnessing a memorable sunrise over the lakes, begin your descent, dropping back down into the shelter of the upper forest.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet forest clearing campsite at 3200m.\n- Day 6 — Day 6 - Descend (2300m, 10 km)\n  Continue descending rapidly, passing through traditional Tamang farming hamlets.\n  *Camp 1*: A warm, comfortable village campsite at 2300m.\n- Day 7 — Return to base (1400m, 8 km + drive)\n  The final easy walk brings you back to the roadhead for your vehicle transport back to Kathmandu.\n  *Panch Pokhari Base*: Complete your trek and celebrate your high-altitude pilgrimage completion.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Panch Pokhari: The Five Sacred Lakes", "rara-lake": "## Rara Lake\n- **ID**: `rara-lake`\n- **Location**: Mugu, Karnali, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 2990m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Trek to Nepal's largest and most pristine body of water, a shimmering, cobalt-blue jewel hidden amidst the remote, pine-forested wilderness of the far-western Mugu district.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Reaching Rara Lake, the largest and deepest freshwater lake in Nepal\n- Deep immersion in the exceptionally remote, untouristed Mugu district\n- Trekking through pristine, scent-filled forests of blue pine and spruce\n- Abundant opportunities to spot rare local wildlife and migratory birds\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Rara Lake base (1500m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Fly into the remote western airstrip of Talcha or drive to the trailhead. Step onto the path, leaving the modern world completely behind.\n  *Rara Lake Base*: Talcha or Gamgadhi, the isolated regional gateway towns of the remote far-west.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (2096m, 8 km)\n  The trail climbs gently through traditional farming hamlets, with the air cooling as you enter the dense coniferous forests.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet forest campsite or simple rustic lodge at 2096m.\n- Day 3 — Day 3 - Ascend (2394m, 7 km)\n  Climb through pristine forests of giant blue pine and spruce. The scent of pine needles fills the crisp mountain air.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 2394m, surrounded by deep, quiet wilderness.\n- Day 4 — Day 4 - Ascend (2692m, 7 km)\n  A steady upward walk along scenic ridges, with spectacular glimpses of the surrounding Karnali hills.\n  *Camp 4*: A beautiful alpine campsite at 2692m, nestled in the woods.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (2990m, 6 km)\n  The climax of the trek. You emerge from the forest to see the massive, cobalt-blue expanse of Rara Lake sitting beautifully at 2990m, reflecting the snow-capped peaks.\n  *Rara Lake High Camp*: Stand on the peaceful shores of Nepal's largest lake, a pristine sanctuary of water and sky.\n- Day 6 — Day 6 - Descend (2692m, 12 km)\n  Begin your return journey, tracing the opposite shores of the lake before dropping back into the forest.\n  *Camp 4*: A quiet forest camp at 2692m.\n- Day 7 — Day 7 - Descend (2394m, 10 km)\n  Descend back through the beautiful coniferous forests, enjoying the quiet trails.\n  *Camp 3*: A peaceful evening camp at 2394m.\n- Day 8 — Day 8 - Descend (2096m, 9 km)\n  Pass through isolated Thakuri and Magar villages, experiencing the unique culture of western Nepal.\n  *Camp 2*: A culturally warm evening in a local village at 2096m.\n- Day 9 — Day 9 - Descend (1798m, 8 km)\n  Continue descending into the warmer lower valleys, walking alongside rushing mountain streams.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail at 1798m.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (1500m, 8 km + drive)\n  Complete the final stretch of your walk back to the roadhead or airstrip.\n  *Rara Lake Base*: Talcha, where you celebrate your journey to Nepal's wild western jewel.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Rara Lake: Nepal's Pristine Western Jewel", "rumtse-tsomoriri": "## Rumtse to Tsomoriri\n- **ID**: `rumtse-tsomoriri`\n- **Location**: Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Difficult | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 5300m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Traverse the high-altitude, wind-sculpted wastes of the Changthang Plateau, crossing five formidable 5,000-meter passes to reach the sacred, sapphire waters of Tsomoriri Lake.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Ascend five challenging passes above 5,000m, including Mandalchan La and Shibuk La\n- Cross the vast, rugged landscapes of the remote Changthang Plateau\n- Witness abundant high-altitude wildlife, including the elusive kiang (wild ass)\n- Reach the breathtaking, sacred waters of Tsomoriri Lake, a Ramsar wetland\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive Leh to Rumtse, Trek to Kyamar Chu (4100m, Drive 3 hrs, Trek 4 km)\n  A scenic drive takes you to the trailhead. Meet your horse crew and ease into the high-altitude terrain with a gentle, acclimatizing walk along the Kyamar stream.\n  *Kyamar Chu*: A remote, basic campsite set beside a high stream, surrounded by the stark beauty of the Ladakhi desert.\n- Day 2 — Kyamar Chu to Tisaling via Kyamari La (5180m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Your first major physical test. Ascend steadily up the rocky trail to crest the Kyamari La (5180m) for sweeping views of the plateau before a gradual descent to camp.\n  *Tisaling*: A sweeping, high-altitude pasture campsite at 4900m, frequented by nomadic herders.\n- Day 3 — Tisaling to Pongunagu via Mandalchan La (5170m, 14 km / Trek)\n  Climb to the second high pass, Mandalchan La (5170m), enjoying the vast, silent trans-Himalayan landscapes before descending toward the salt-flats of Tso Kar.\n  *Pongunagu*: A spacious campsite situated near the shores of Tso Kar Lake, known for its unique birdlife and white salt crusts.\n- Day 4 — Pongunagu to Nuruchan (4580m, 12 km / Trek)\n  An easier, flatter day of walking along the expansive Tso Kar basin. Keep your eyes peeled for herds of kiang running across the plains.\n  *Nuruchan*: A quiet, peaceful campsite located beside a small spring near the lake basin.\n- Day 5 — Nuruchan to Rachungkaru via Horlam La (4900m, 15 km / Trek)\n  Climb steadily over the Horlam La (4900m) pass, dropping into a wide, dry valley where nomadic Changpa families tend their pashmina goats.\n  *Rachungkaru*: A wild, high-altitude campsite at 4600m, offering a fascinating look at the nomadic way of life.\n- Day 6 — Rachungkaru to Gyama Lhoma via Kyamayuri La (5180m, 18 km / Trek)\n  A demanding, long day of trekking across the high plateau. You climb to the crest of Kyamayuri La (5180m), surrounded by endless rocky peaks.\n  *Gyama Lhoma*: An isolated, high camp set in a wide valley, offering incredible, dark starlit skies.\n- Day 7 — Gyama Lhoma to Korzok Phu via Shibuk La (5260m, 15 km / Trek)\n  Tackle the steep, challenging ascent of Shibuk La (5260m), your fifth and final high pass. The summit offers a spectacular panorama of the Rupshu valley.\n  *Korzok Phu*: A seasonal nomadic winter settlement, lively with yaks and traditional black-tent encampments.\n- Day 8 — Korzok Phu to Kyungdo via Parang La Traverse (5300m, 14 km / Trek)\n  Walk through high, glacial valleys on the approaches to the historic Parang La trail, navigating rugged moraines.\n  *Kyungdo*: A remote, high-altitude wilderness campsite offering spectacular evening light over the peaks.\n- Day 9 — Kyungdo to Karzok (Tsomoriri Lake) (4522m, 12 km / Trek)\n  The final descent. The deep, cobalt-blue expanse of Tsomoriri Lake slowly reveals itself, a breathtaking reward after days of desert trekking.\n  *Karzok*: The only permanent settlement on the lake shores, home to the ancient Karzok Monastery.\n- Day 10 — Karzok to Leh (3500m, Drive 6-7 hrs)\n  Board your vehicle for the long, scenic drive back along the Indus River valley to Leh.\n  *Leh*: Return to the capital of Ladakh, where a hot shower and proper bed await.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Rumtse to Tsomoriri: The Changthang Wilderness", "sailung": "## Sailung Trek\n- **ID**: `sailung`\n- **Location**: Kathmandu Valley, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 3 days | **Max Altitude**: 3146m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Ascend a gentle, sacred hilltop of a hundred rolling grassy mounds, enjoying an incredibly wide, crowd-free panorama of the central Himalayan range.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Panoramic views of the Annapurna, Langtang, and Everest ranges\n- Exploring the sacred, rolling grassy mounds of Sailung Hill\n- An easy, low-altitude 3-day trek close to Kathmandu\n- Rich cultural immersion in local Tamang and Sherpa villages\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Sailung base (1400m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A scenic drive from Kathmandu brings you to the starting point. Step onto the trail for a gentle introductory walk through terraced farmlands.\n  *Sailung Base*: Mudhe or a nearby village, a quiet middle-hills settlement serving as your starting gateway.\n- Day 2 — Summit / High Point (3146m, 6 km)\n  A gentle, scenic climb up through rhododendron forests brings you to the top of Sailung Hill. The hilltop is a unique, grassy landscape of a hundred rolling mounds, sacred to locals.\n  *Sailung High Camp*: The panoramic summit at 3146m, offering an exceptionally wide, unobstructed view of the Himalayas.\n- Day 3 — Return to base (1400m, 8 km + drive)\n  Wake up for a spectacular sunrise over the peaks. Descend back down the grassy hills, passing local shrines before your vehicle transport back to Kathmandu.\n  *Sailung Base*: Complete your hike and board your vehicle, reflecting on a peaceful, scenic getaway.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Sailung Trek: The Hill of a Hundred Mounds", "shey-phoksundo": "## Shey Phoksundo\n- **ID**: `shey-phoksundo`\n- **Location**: Dolpo, Karnali, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 10 days | **Max Altitude**: 3660m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 11\n\n**Description**: Journey deep into the remote, ancient land of Dolpo, tracing wild pine forests and deep gorges to stand before the impossibly blue, sacred waters of Phoksundo Lake.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Reaching Shey Phoksundo, the deepest and most spectacular lake in Nepal\n- Deep immersion in the ancient, pre-Buddhist Bon culture of Dolpo\n- Trekking through the wild, untouched forests of Phoksundo National Park\n- Spectacular views of soaring, barren peaks and deep river gorges\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Shey Phoksundo base (2000m, Drive + 3 km)\n  Fly into the remote mountain airstrip of Juphal. Step onto the trail, immediately entering the wild, isolated valleys of Dolpo.\n  *Shey Phoksundo Base*: Juphal or Dunai, the isolated regional gateway towns of the far-west.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (2664m, 7 km)\n  The trail climbs gently along the roaring Bheri River, passing through small pine forests and local farming hamlets.\n  *Camp 2*: A quiet, riverside village campsite or simple lodge at 2664m.\n- Day 3 — Day 3 - Ascend (2996m, 6 km)\n  Walk deeper into the national park. The trail steepens as you climb through dense pine and fir forests, with the air cooling.\n  *Camp 3*: Set up camp at 2996m, surrounded by deep, quiet wilderness.\n- Day 4 — Day 4 - Ascend (3328m, 10 km)\n  A longer day of steady climbing along the river gorge, crossing high suspension bridges suspended above the roaring waters.\n  *Camp 4*: A beautiful alpine campsite at 3328m, nestled in the woods.\n- Day 5 — Summit / High Point (3660m, 6 km)\n  The climax of the trek. Climb up a prominent ridge to emerge beside the spectacular, impossibly blue expanse of Phoksundo Lake, sitting beautifully at 3660m.\n  *Shey Phoksundo High Camp*: Ringmo village on the lake shores, a historic Bon-Buddhist settlement of flat-roofed mud houses.\n- Day 6 — Day 6 - Descend (3328m, 9 km)\n  Begin your return journey, descending down the steep valley pathways from the lake basin.\n  *Camp 4*: Return to the comfortable, forested camp at 3328m.\n- Day 7 — Day 7 - Descend (2996m, 10 km)\n  Continue descending back through the deep coniferous forests, enjoying the quiet trails.\n  *Camp 3*: A peaceful evening camp at 2996m.\n- Day 8 — Day 8 - Descend (2664m, 9 km)\n  Pass through isolated local villages, experiencing the unique culture of western Nepal.\n  *Camp 2*: A culturally warm evening in a local village at 2664m.\n- Day 9 — Day 9 - Descend (2332m, 6 km)\n  Continue descending into the warmer lower valleys, walking alongside rushing mountain streams.\n  *Camp 1*: Your final night on the trail at 2332m.\n- Day 10 — Return to base (2000m, 8 km + drive)\n  Complete the final stretch of your walk back to the Juphal airstrip to catch your flight out.\n  *Shey Phoksundo Base*: Juphal, where you celebrate your successful journey to Nepal's turquoise western jewel.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Shey Phoksundo: The Turquoise Jewel of Dolpo", "snow-lake": "## Snow Lake Trek\n- **ID**: `snow-lake`\n- **Location**: Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 12 days | **Max Altitude**: 5100m\n- **Best Months**: 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Embark on an extraordinary, expedition-grade traverse of the immense Lungmoche Glacier, crossing the formidable 5,100m Thanglasgo Pass to reach the mystical, snow-bound basin of Snow Lake.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Traverse the formidable Thanglasgo Pass (5100m) – the highest point of the trek\n- Experience extensive, challenging glacier trekking across the immense Lungmoche Glacier\n- Reach the mystical Snow Lake plateau, a massive snow-bound glacial basin\n- Immerse yourself in the profound remoteness of the Karakoram range\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrival in Leh & Acclimatization (3500m, Drive 0.5 hrs)\n  Arrive at Leh airport and transfer to your guesthouse. Spend the day resting and allowing your body to adjust to the high altitude. Hydrate constantly.\n  *Leh*: The capital of Ladakh, a bustling town at 3500m serving as the gateway to the region.\n- Day 2 — Leh Acclimatization & Local Sightseeing (3500m, Walk 2-3 km)\n  Another day dedicated to acclimatization. Visit Shanti Stupa for panoramic views of Leh and the Indus Valley, or explore Leh Palace and Tsemo Gompa.\n  *Leh*: Still in the vibrant capital, focusing on exploring its cultural heritage and ensuring proper acclimatization.\n- Day 3 — Drive to Warshi, Trek to Phurphu (Nubra Valley) (3400m, Drive 6-7 hrs, Trek 2-3 km)\n  A scenic drive takes you over Khardung La into the Nubra Valley. From Warshi, the last village, begin your trek, following the Shyok River to camp.\n  *Phurphu*: A remote camping ground near the Shyok River, serving as your entry point into the complete wilderness.\n- Day 4 — Trek to Saktung (3550m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Your trek continues along the Shyok River through increasingly remote, barren landscapes. The trail is mostly flat with gradual ascents.\n  *Saktung*: A desolate, high-altitude campsite along the river, marking your deeper penetration into the trek.\n- Day 5 — Trek to Langpoche (3700m, 10 km / Trek)\n  The trail presents a mix of riverside walking and negotiating initial moraines. You'll start to see more dramatic rock formations and the immense scale of the mountains.\n  *Langpoche*: A high-altitude camping site where the terrain begins to show more signs of glacial activity.\n- Day 6 — Trek to Camp I (Lungmoche Glacier Base) (4000m, 8 km / Trek)\n  Begin to leave the riverbed and ascend onto the lateral moraines of the Lungmoche Glacier. The terrain becomes rougher, requiring careful foot placement.\n  *Camp I*: Your first true high-altitude glacial base camp, situated directly at the snout of the massive glacier.\n- Day 7 — Trek across Lungmoche Glacier to Camp II (4400m, 10-12 km / Trek)\n  A challenging day of glacier travel. Depending on conditions, crampons might be necessary. Navigate the sprawling glacier to establish Camp II.\n  *Camp II*: A high-altitude camp situated directly adjacent to the vast expanse of the Lungmoche Glacier.\n- Day 8 — Summit Thanglasgo Pass & Trek to Snow Lake (4700m (Pass: 5100m), 12-14 km / Trek)\n  The crux of the trek. An early start to tackle the ascent to Thanglasgo Pass (5100m) for panoramic views of the Karakoram, before descending to Snow Lake.\n  *Snow Lake*: A magnificent, vast glacial basin, surrounded by towering peaks—a truly remote and pristine camping experience.\n- Day 9 — Return Trek from Snow Lake to Camp II (via Thanglasgo Pass) (4400m (Pass: 5100m), 12-14 km / Trek)\n  Retrace your steps, once again crossing back over Thanglasgo Pass. The descent back towards Camp II requires careful navigation on the glacier.\n  *Camp II*: Returning to your previous glacial campsite, a wild haven after successfully traversing the pass.\n- Day 10 — Trek from Camp II to Panamik Hot Springs (3200m, 20-22 km / Trek)\n  A long day as you exit the main glacial system. Descend from Camp II, eventually rejoining the more defined trails leading back toward civilization.\n  *Panamik*: A village in the Nubra Valley famous for its natural hot sulfur springs, offering a therapeutic end to your strenuous trek.\n- Day 11 — Drive back to Leh (3500m, Drive 5-6 hrs)\n  After a hearty breakfast, begin your scenic drive back over Khardung La to Leh. Enjoy a proper bed and hot shower.\n  *Leh*: Back in the familiar comfort of Leh, relaxing after the arduous but exhilarating trek.\n- Day 12 — Departure from Leh (3500m, Drive 0.5 hrs)\n  Transfer to Leh airport for your onward journey, carrying memories of an unforgettable expedition into the Karakoram.\n  *Leh*: Your departure point, marking the end of your extraordinary journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Snow Lake Trek: The Karakoram Traverse", "spiti-valley": "## Spiti Valley Trek\n- **ID**: `spiti-valley`\n- **Location**: Spiti, Himachal Pradesh, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 8 days | **Max Altitude**: 4550m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Journey through the vast, wind-sculpted cold desert of Spiti, visiting ancient cliffside Buddhist monasteries and the highest inhabited villages in the world.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Experience the stark, dramatic high-altitude cold desert of Spiti\n- Visit ancient cliffside monasteries like Key and Tabo\n- Explore the world's highest villages connected by road, including Hikkim and Komic\n- Camp on the shores of the shimmering, crescent-shaped Chandratal Lake\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Manali to Kaza (Arrival Day) (3800m, 200 km / Drive)\n  Embark on a long, spectacular drive from Manali over the Rohtang Pass into the Spiti Valley, arriving in Kaza to rest and acclimatize.\n  *Kaza*: The administrative capital of Spiti, a bustling town at 3800m surrounded by towering, barren mountains.\n- Day 2 — Kaza to Langza, Hikkim, Komik, and return to Kaza (4400m, 50 km / Drive)\n  Explore the highest inhabited villages in the world. Visit Langza with its iconic Buddha statue, and send a postcard from Hikkim, home to the world's highest post office.\n  *Kaza*: Return to your base in Kaza, feeling the effects of active acclimatization.\n- Day 3 — Kaza to Tabo, Dhankar, Pin Valley, Mudh, and return to Kaza (3280m, 120 km / Drive)\n  Explore the ancient, 1000-year-old Tabo Monastery, and visit Dhankar Monastery perched precariously on a cliff edge before driving into the picturesque Pin Valley.\n  *Kaza*: Return to Kaza, having experienced a rich day of cultural and geographical exploration.\n- Day 4 — Kaza to Ki Monastery, Kibber, Chicham Bridge, and return to Kaza (4160m, 40 km / Drive)\n  Visit the iconic, multi-tiered Ki Monastery, a major learning center for Buddhism, and cross the spectacular Chicham Bridge, one of the highest in Asia.\n  *Kaza*: Spend your final evening in Kaza, preparing for your journey to the high lake.\n- Day 5 — Kaza to Chandratal Lake (4300m, 80 km / Drive)\n  Embark on a scenic drive to the mesmerizing Chandratal Lake, also known as the \"Moon Lake,\" setting up camp near its shores.\n  *Chandratal*: A spectacular crescent-shaped lake sitting beautifully at 4300m, surrounded by glaciers and high peaks.\n- Day 6 — Chandratal Lake to Batal, Kunzum Pass, and return to Kaza (4551m, 80 km / Drive)\n  Enjoy a peaceful sunrise at the lake before driving back, crossing the majestic Kunzum Pass (4551m) which offers panoramic mountain views.\n  *Kaza*: Return to Kaza for a comfortable evening.\n- Day 7 — Kaza to Reckong Peo (2290m, 200 km / Drive)\n  Bid farewell to Spiti and drive toward Reckong Peo in the Kinnaur district, enjoying the dramatic transition as you descend.\n  *Reckong Peo*: A bustling town in Kinnaur, offering spectacular views of the sacred Kinner Kailash peak.\n- Day 8 — Reckong Peo to Shimla (Departure Day) (2276m, 230 km / Drive)\n  Conclude your Spiti adventure with a long, scenic drive along the Sutlej River to the historic hill station of Shimla.\n  *Shimla*: The capital of Himachal Pradesh, marking the end of your extraordinary journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Spiti Valley: The Middle Land", "stok-kangri": "## Stok Kangri\n- **ID**: `stok-kangri`\n- **Location**: Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Challenging | **Duration**: 9 days | **Max Altitude**: 6153m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Stand on the summit of Ladakh's iconic, towering Stok Kangri peak, conquering a demanding, non-technical climb to over 6,000 meters with sweeping views of the Karakoram.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Summit Ladakh's iconic Stok Kangri peak at 6153m\n- Capture panoramic views of the Zanskar and Karakoram ranges\n- Challenge yourself with a physically demanding non-technical climb\n- Experience the ultimate sunrise vantage point at over 6,000 meters\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive Leh to Stok Village & Trek to Chang Ma (3980m, Drive 1 hr, Trek 6 km)\n  A short drive brings you to the trailhead. Step onto the path, climbing steadily up a dry, rocky valley to your first high campsite.\n  *Chang Ma*: A quiet, stony campsite at 3980m, nestled in a narrow Ladakhi gorge.\n- Day 2 — Chang Ma to Mankorma (4330m, 4 km / Trek)\n  A short, gentle climb to aid acclimatization. The trail follows the stream bed, surrounded by the barren, colorful mountains of the Stok range.\n  *Mankorma*: A beautiful, open pasture campsite at 4330m, offering a serene evening under the stars.\n- Day 3 — Mankorma to Base Camp (4980m, 4 km / Trek)\n  The trail climbs steadily up a prominent ridge, with the air cooling rapidly as you approach the high camp.\n  *Stok Base Camp*: A sprawling, lively base camp at 4980m, situated on a grassy meadow beneath the towering peak.\n- Day 4 — Acclimatization & Training at Base Camp (4980m)\n  A vital rest day. Your guides will conduct a training session, practicing walking on snow and ice with crampons and utilizing safety ropes.\n  *Stok Base Camp*: Rest and hydrate, preparing mentally and physically for the midnight summit push.\n- Day 5 — Summit Stok Kangri Peak & Return to Base Camp (6153m, 12 km / Trek)\n  Wake at 11:00 PM. Tackle a steep scramble up the moraine, strap on crampons for the glacier, and climb the final, exposed ridge. Reach the summit (6153m) as the sun ignites the Karakoram, before a careful descent.\n  *Stok Base Camp*: Return to the safety of your tent, exhausted but triumphant.\n- Day 6 — Base Camp to Stok Village & Drive to Leh (3500m, 14 km Trek + Drive)\n  A long, downhill trek back through Mankorma and Chang Ma to Stok Village. Board your vehicle for the drive back to Leh.\n  *Leh*: Return to the capital, enjoying a hot shower, a proper bed, and a celebratory meal.\n- Day 7 — Buffer Day in Leh (3500m)\n  An essential buffer day in case bad weather, wind, or snow delays the summit attempt on Day 5.\n  *Leh*: Spend a relaxing day visiting local monasteries or resting.\n- Day 8 — Leisure Day in Leh (3500m)\n  A leisurely day to explore Leh market, buy souvenirs, or enjoy the local cafes.\n  *Leh*: Relax and soak in the Ladakhi atmosphere.\n- Day 9 — Departure from Leh (3500m, Drive 0.5 hrs)\n  Transfer to Leh airport for your flight back to Delhi, carrying memories of an unforgettable 6,000-meter climb.\n  *Leh*: Your final departure point.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Stok Kangri: Conquering the 6,000m Giant", "tamang-heritage": "## Tamang Heritage Trail\n- **ID**: `tamang-heritage`\n- **Location**: Langtang, Bagmati, Nepal\n- **Difficulty**: Easy | **Duration**: 6 days | **Max Altitude**: 3165m\n- **Best Months**: 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 12\n\n**Description**: Immerse yourself in the rich, vibrant culture of the Tamang people, walking through terraced hillsides and traditional villages bordering the Langtang National Park.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Immersive cultural encounters with the welcoming Tamang people\n- Staying in authentic, community-run village homestays\n- Spectacular views of the Ganesh Himal and Langtang ranges\n- Relaxing in natural hot springs at Tatopani along the trail\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Arrive at Tamang Heritage base (1400m, Drive + 3 km)\n  A scenic drive from Kathmandu brings you to the starting point. Step onto the trail, leaving the city behind for the green, rolling foothills.\n  *Tamang Heritage Base*: Syabrubesi, a bustling gateway village serving as the entrance to the Langtang region.\n- Day 2 — Day 2 - Ascend (2577m, 11 km)\n  Climb steadily through beautiful terraced farmlands and ancient forests of oak and rhododendron, passing through small Tamang hamlets.\n  *Camp 2*: Gatlang, a large, traditional Tamang village famous for its woodcarvings and stone houses.\n- Day 3 — Summit / High Point (3165m, 6 km)\n  A spectacular climb up the ridge brings you to your high point, offering panoramic, close-up views of the Ganesh Himal and Langtang ranges.\n  *Tamang Heritage High Camp*: Nagthali, a beautiful high-pasture viewpoint perched at 3165m.\n- Day 4 — Day 4 - Descend (2577m, 6 km)\n  Descend from the high ridge, passing through the village of Thuman, famous for its warm hospitality and traditional culture.\n  *Camp 2*: A comfortable village homestay at 2577m, enjoying a hearty home-cooked meal.\n- Day 5 — Day 5 - Descend (1988m, 7 km)\n  Continue descending down through the valley, passing through Briddim, another beautiful Tamang village with strong Tibetan influence.\n  *Camp 1*: A cozy, traditional homestay at 1988m.\n- Day 6 — Return to base (1400m, 8 km + drive)\n  A gentle walk along the river brings you back to the roadhead for your vehicle transport back to Kathmandu.\n  *Tamang Heritage Base*: Syabrubesi. Your beautiful, 6-day cultural journey is complete.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Tamang Heritage Trail: Cultural Tapestry of Langtang", "tarsar-marsar": "## Tarsar Marsar\n- **ID**: `tarsar-marsar`\n- **Location**: Kashmir, Jammu & Kashmir, India\n- **Difficulty**: Moderate | **Duration**: 7 days | **Max Altitude**: 3900m\n- **Best Months**: 6, 7, 8, 9\n\n**Description**: Discover the legendary twin alpine lakes of Kashmir, walking through lush green valleys, pine forests, and wildflower meadows nestled beneath the towering glaciers of the Kolahoi massif.\n\n**Highlights**:\n- Trek past the legendary twin alpine lakes of Tarsar and Marsar\n- Cross the spectacular and challenging Tarsar Pass at 3900m\n- Camp in the vast, lush green meadows of Lidderwat and Shekwas\n- Experience the raw, untamed beauty of the Kashmiri wilderness\n\n**Itinerary**:\n- Day 1 — Drive Srinagar to Aru Valley & Trek to Riverside (2400m, Drive 4 hrs, Trek 3 km)\n  Drive from Srinagar along the roaring Lidder River to the beautiful Aru Valley. Step onto the trail, walking a short distance through pine forests to camp.\n  *Aru Valley*: A picturesque, riverside village acting as the traditional starting gateway of the trek, surrounded by towering pines.\n- Day 2 — Aru to Lidderwat (2780m, 10 km / Trek)\n  The trail climbs steadily along the roaring Lidder River. You wind through dense pine and fir forests, emerging into a wide, beautiful alpine meadow.\n  *Lidderwat*: A sprawling, emerald-green meadow campsite at 2780m, flanked by whispering pine forests and mountain streams.\n- Day 3 — Lidderwat to Shekwas (3350m, 6 km / Trek)\n  A gentle climb up through alpine pastures, passing local Gujjar shepherd camps. The tree canopy breaks to reveal spectacular views of the snow peaks.\n  *Shekwas*: A peaceful, high-altitude alpine meadow at 3350m, offering a serene campsite near the glacial streams.\n- Day 4 — Shekwas to Tarsar Lake (3790m, 5 km / Trek)\n  A short, spectacular climb. You navigate rocky trails to emerge beside the breathtaking, turquoise-blue expanse of Tarsar Lake, surrounded by rock walls.\n  *Tarsar Lake*: A massive, pristine alpine lake at 3790m, offering one of the most beautiful camping locations in the world.\n- Day 5 — Tarsar to Sundersar Lake via Tarsar Pass (3900m, 5 km / Trek)\n  Cross the spectacular Tarsar Pass (3900m), your highest point. Descend into a beautiful, hidden valley to reach the peaceful waters of Sundersar.\n  *Sundersar Lake*: A quiet, high-altitude alpine lake nestled in a dramatic, rocky basin beneath the glaciers.\n- Day 6 — Sundersar to Marsar Lake & Descend to Homwas (3500m, 9 km / Trek)\n  Cross a small ridge to stand overlooking Marsar Lake, the companion to Tarsar. After taking in the views, begin a steady descent to Homwas.\n  *Homwas*: A comfortable meadow campsite at 3500m, enjoying the thicker air.\n- Day 7 — Homwas to Aru & Drive to Srinagar (2400m, 13 km Trek + Drive)\n  The final long descent. You plunge down a steep, pine-forested ridge, dropping rapidly to the Aru Valley where your vehicle waits.\n  *Srinagar*: Complete your trek and return to the capital, celebrating your successful Kashmiri lakes journey.\n\n**Long-form editorial content**:\n# Tarsar Marsar: Kashmir's Twin Lakes"};

Deno.serve(async (req) => {
  if (req.method === "OPTIONS") return new Response(null, { headers: corsHeaders });
  const supabase = createClient(
    Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_URL")!,
    Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY")!,
  );
  const results: Record<string, string> = {};
  for (const [trek_id, content] of Object.entries(TREKS)) {
    const { data: existing } = await supabase
      .from("trek_overrides")
      .select("id")
      .eq("trek_id", trek_id)
      .maybeSingle();
    const patch = {
      long_form_content: content,
      content_source: "ai_generated",
      updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),
    };
    if (existing) {
      const { error } = await supabase.from("trek_overrides").update(patch).eq("id", existing.id);
      results[trek_id] = error ? `err: ${error.message}` : "updated";
    } else {
      const { error } = await supabase.from("trek_overrides").insert({ ...patch, trek_id });
      results[trek_id] = error ? `err: ${error.message}` : "inserted";
    }
  }
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, count: Object.keys(TREKS).length, results }), {
    headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  });
});
